noi_dung:
{align:justify|Professor Quan and his wife are now 55 years old. They have three daughters of remarkable beauty, quite far apart in age: 25, 23, and 20. Though these young ladies are as lovely as jade and gold, not one of them has ever had a suitor. The professors are beside themselves with grief. Many young men fall head over heels the moment they set eyes on the girls and pursue them ardently — but as soon as they discover that these peerless beauties cannot utter a word, they immediately retreat and walk away.}
{align:justify|Professor Quan and his wife belong to the educated upper class and never believed in the law of cause and effect. Yet having given birth one after another to three mute daughters, their spirits were battered almost to collapse. They were stunned and bewildered, filled with immeasurable suffering. Sorrow aged their faces; though highly learned and wealthy, they could bring no happiness to their own children. The affliction of the three beauties filled onlookers with awe, concern, and unanswerable questions.}
{align:justify|There is a saying: "In youth people love to discuss learning; in middle age they love to discuss destiny; in old age they love to discuss religion." Professor Quan and his wife are now entering old age, and their enduring anguish is a sickness no medicine can cure. So gradually they began to develop an interest in religion.}
{align:justify|One weekend, at a friend's encouragement, they went to a suburban temple to listen to an eminent monk expound the Dharma.}
{align:justify|The Venerable Master taught: "Good deeds bring good rewards, evil deeds bring evil retribution." The law of cause and effect exists with complete objectivity — it was not decreed or invented by the Buddha. The Buddha merely proclaimed it, explaining it clearly so that sentient beings would be warned and vigilant. Whether you believe it or not, all of our lives unfold within cause and effect, so each of us must take responsibility for our own good and evil actions. Every person is the creator of his own destiny. If you do evil yourself, you will meet with miserable retribution...}
{align:justify|The theme of that day's Dharma talk seemed to rise up precisely for Mr. Quan to contemplate and to confront the depths of his own heart... Suddenly he recalled something from 25 years before...}
{align:justify|"Back then, Professor Quan and his wife had been married only two months when Mrs. Quan became pregnant. Mr. Quan was overjoyed and drove his wife in an expensive luxury car to a scenic coastal region for a holiday.}
{align:justify|On one occasion they visited northern Thailand to attend a banquet, parking the car in the wide courtyard of a temple. The moment Mr. Quan opened the car door, a crowd of ragged street children rushed over, jostling and tugging at their sleeves, begging for money.}
{align:justify|Professor Quan despised beggars. Not only did he give nothing himself, he announced to everyone around:}
{align:justify|"Don't give them money. That only trains them in bad habits — refusing to earn an honest living, idling about on charity, and becoming a blight on society..."}
{align:justify|Hearing this, the vagrant children drifted away. After the banquet, when the Quans returned to their parking spot, they discovered that their beloved brand-new luxury car had been slashed with a deep scratch running from front to back. Professor Quan erupted; blazing with fury, he assumed it must have been those street children. Scanning the surroundings, he spotted four or five children aged about 13 or 14 gathered under a nearby mango tree. He charged over and, without asking who was right or wrong, slapped every one of them hard across the face. Then he loudly demanded they confess who had scratched his precious car. When no one would own up, he threatened to haul them to the police station. Terrified, the oldest of them pointed into the distance — at a ragged little beggar boy who was begging from some foreigners — and said he was the culprit. Still raging, Professor Quan stormed over, seized the boy, and dragged him to the car, while the other children seized the chance to scatter and escape.}
{align:justify|Professor Quan savagely slapped both cheeks of the child and fiercely interrogated him: why had he dared to scratch the car? Only after questioning him for some time did he discover something: the boy was mute. The child kept gesturing helplessly with his hands, tears brimming in his terrified eyes, his gaze utterly pitiful. Professor Quan could not understand sign language and concluded the boy must have borne a grudge over his "give them no money" remark and vandalized the car out of spite. So in fury he lashed out with his right foot, kicking the mute boy hard in the chest. With a dull thud the boy toppled over, legs in the air — and then, with a retching sound, bright red blood gushed from his mouth. The bystanders rushed in, pulled the professor away, and carried the mute child off so he would be beaten no more.}
{align:justify|As the mute boy was led away, he kept turning back to glare at the professor, his eyes full of hatred. Afterward the mute child took shelter in the temple; he fell gravely ill and within a few years became disabled. Everyone knew the mute boy was good-natured at heart — he had never scratched the car. Professor Quan had beaten an innocent child.}
{align:justify|At the end of that year, Mrs. Quan gave birth to a baby girl with delicate brows and bright eyes — but by age three she still could not speak. Then Mrs. Quan bore a second daughter, who likewise could not speak by age three. Finally she bore a third daughter, and the moment she was born they hurriedly called a doctor to examine her: yet another mute little girl! Fearing that a fourth or fifth child would also be a mute daughter, Mrs. Quan asked the doctor to sterilize her."}
{align:justify|Now, every time Professor Quan looks at his three mute daughters, he recalls that merciless kick he dealt the mute boy long ago. He cannot forget the sight of bright blood spurting from the boy's mouth, nor that terrifying, hate-filled stare...}
{align:justify|Even today the memory makes him shudder. He has come to understand and can no longer disbelieve the principle of karmic retribution — the law of Heaven that turns in perfect fairness, a law of nature itself. For that brutal act of 25 years ago, regret now comes far too late.}
{align:justify|Since then, every weekend Professor Quan and his wife go to the temple to hear the eminent monks expound the sutras, repenting their sins before the Buddha. They have vowed to cultivate goodness and accumulate virtue. As the proverb says: "Only at fifty does a man finally see the faults of his forty-ninth year."}
{align:justify|Commentary:}
{align:justify|Having translated this story, I am reminded of a tale of cause and effect from the Tang dynasty:}
{align:justify|There was a farmer of extremely cruel nature. One morning he went out to inspect his fields and saw his neighbor's water buffalo eating his rice shoots and trampling his paddies into a mess. Exploding with rage, he seized the buffalo by the head, drew a sharp knife, and cut out the animal's tongue.}
{align:justify|Ten years later the farmer married and had three children — but all of them were mute. He sought famous physicians everywhere to cure his children, all in vain. He could not understand why his children were like this, and his heart ached beyond measure. One night he dreamed of the scene in which he had cruelly cut out the buffalo's tongue. Waking, he understood everything: because of that evil deed, his children now suffered this calamity. In truth, it was only right that the farmer himself should bear the bitter retribution — but why were his children also afflicted? This can only be explained in Buddhist terms as collective karma: those who commit similar evil together will receive the same retribution together.}

{align:justify|In a city in northern Thailand there was a noodle shop famed for its extraordinarily delicious broth. Its fragrance spread far and wide, and everyone flocked to eat there. The gourmets of the day all sang its praises. So although the shop worked only in the mornings, it sold several hundred bowls every morning and was sold out clean before noon. Each bowl cost about 15 to 20 Thai baht. Daily takings came to some 6,000, with 3,000 profit.}
{align:justify|The shop had started as a small roadside stall, but as profits grew, within just a few short years the owners bought land and built a spacious new restaurant. Behind it lay a large flower garden — to rest there on a quiet afternoon after the morning trade was truly paradise on earth.}
{align:justify|The owners, surnamed Lien, were a couple over sixty with both sons and daughters, all of them married. The whole family of five joined forces in the business and hired no outside help. Their broth tasted far superior to any rival's; connoisseurs were enraptured at the first sip. So from high officials and merchants down to market vendors and cyclo drivers, all became long-standing regulars.}
{align:justify|Rival shops could not master the recipe and tried in vain to pry out the trade secret. As the saying goes, "fellows of a trade are foes" — and it is no lie. Failing to extract the secret of the broth, the rival shops spread a spiteful rumor: "That shop puts opium in its broth — eat it once and you're hooked; you have to have it every day, you can't do without it."}
{align:justify|Someone went further still, quietly and secretly reporting to the Health Department, firmly insisting that the broth contained opium.}
{align:justify|Opium is a banned poison, so the Health Department at once sent inspectors to test the shop. In the end their conclusion was: "There is no opium or harmful chemical of any kind. The broth fully matches the owner's own account — it is made from vegetable juices combined with chicken stock and pork bones, simmered over a low flame for a full day and night to reach the required standard."}
{align:justify|Cleared and certified by the Health Department as having "NOTHING" in it, the shop grew more famous than ever and more beloved than ever — without spending a penny, it had been advertised for free, and the fame of its broth flew ever farther...}
{align:justify|There was a prince of the Thai royal family, a renowned culinary expert, who drove to the shop to taste it. Afterward he awarded it a medal and a special citation to hang before the door for all to admire. With this "golden plaque" from the prince, the owners' prestige rose higher still: each bowl of noodles went from 15 baht up to 20 or 25, and the Lien family's business prospered more than ever.}
{align:justify|Nothing in this world stays secret forever, and in the end the Lien family's jealously guarded broth monopoly was exposed.}
{align:justify|The one who lifted the veil on the secret was old Lien himself, and this strange and unprecedented news shook all of northeastern Thailand.}
{align:justify|The first to discover the secret was a cyclo driver named Tung. That day at lunch, Tung had drunk so much wine that his face was flushed and he could not take passengers; having no money to hand over to his wife, he dared not go home. That night, with his stomach growling, he slipped in through the shop's back door, thinking to scrounge something to line his belly.}
{align:justify|When he had pried open the back door and stepped into the kitchen, dim light glowed from afar — just enough for him to see someone sitting with his back to the door, which is to say, facing him. Looking closer, he realized it was the shop owner at work. Tung boldly crept nearer for a peek and saw clearly: old Lien was killing frogs. The old man's technique for slitting bellies and removing innards was masterful. He sorted everything into plastic tubs and plastic bags; the tubs were filled with frogs' corpses.}
{align:justify|Just then the owner seemed to sense someone and suddenly turned to look toward the door. Tung hid himself in time, and thanks to the dim light he was not spotted.}
{align:justify|Though the owner did not see Tung, Tung, hidden in the darkness, saw old Lien's face with perfect clarity in the lamplight. At once he nearly fainted with fright: the owner's face was not a human face at all, but a huge frog's face. The mouth was exactly like a frog's, the eyes bulged, and the sounds he made were a bizarre "krek-krek." Suddenly Tung saw him stick out a long tongue and snap it back in — exactly like a frog catching prey. Then he quickly withdrew inside.}
{align:justify|Tung was so terrified he wanted to scream and run for his life, but his legs only trembled and would not obey him. With great difficulty he pulled himself together and crawled out the door.}
{align:justify|That night he was so frightened he came down with a raging fever. Days later, recovered, he told everyone everything he had witnessed that night.}
{align:justify|But since he was known as a drinker, no one would believe a word he said. In truth, to people in northeastern Thailand, eating frogs, rats, cats and the like is commonplace — nothing strange about it.}
{align:justify|What was strange about the rumor was the honorable owner's face. For months now, diners had no longer seen old Lien show himself. Where he used to sit at the front of the shop chopping chicken, now word was he was sick in hospital, and no one knew when he would be discharged. If anyone did catch sight of him, it was only a fleeting glimpse; lately he seldom stepped into the shop.}
{align:justify|Those who happened to meet him reported:}
{align:justify|"The old owner's face looks exactly like a frog's — only much bigger. His legs are bent like a frog's too. The mouth is identical, the eyes bulge — the more you look, the more he resembles a giant frog."}
{align:justify|At this, everyone believed Tung had told the truth. Without a word they conspired to spy until they caught sight of the "man turned frog" that their owner had become. And in the end everyone learned that the irresistible broth was frog soup — strangely, improbably sweet and clear.}
{align:justify|Before, fearing the secret would leak, the owner had dared hire no workers and would let no other hand do the work, so he had to kill every frog himself. Over a lifetime old Lien had killed more lives than anyone could count — truly innumerable. Every day he faced the faces of frogs; his every act and gesture was entangled with frogs. As the mind is, so the form becomes; the outward shape follows the heart — and gradually his own face changed into a frog's shape.}
{align:justify|At first those living with him did not notice, until by the time it was discovered he had been utterly transformed. Later, the owner's relatives told how: "Lately, whenever old Lien falls asleep, he screams and roars, wailing that frogs are swarming all over his body, biting and tearing at him. The moment he dozes off they plague him so, and he can never rest in peace."}
{align:justify|From then on, for months on end, every night in bed he was visited by hosts of frogs that tormented him with pain. In the end old Lien went mad, croaking like a frog, and died.}
{align:justify|Afterward, the whole family of that once-famous broth shop vanished, none knows where — leaving behind only this story, a topic people still discuss to this day.}

{align:justify|That day every newspaper carried the same headline: "Young doctor Truong Vi Luc takes his own life"... and readers could hardly believe it.}
{align:justify|Doctor Truong was 29 years old this year; after graduating last year, he had been serving at a government hospital.}
{align:justify|Doctor Truong came from a wealthy family, with kind and good-hearted parents. His three elder sisters were all married, and as the youngest child — and only son — he was doted on ten thousandfold. He was strikingly handsome, a quick-witted charmer who set a great many young hearts aflame.}
{align:justify|With a velvet-paved road stretching out before him, relatives and friends all envied his golden fortune — which is why no one could believe he had killed himself.}
{align:justify|The hospital therefore at once assembled a forensic team to investigate his suicide, starting from Truong Vi Luc's family background and his six years of medical school, right through to his graduation and service at the hospital. The documented file ran to more than 500 pages — proof of how deeply his superiors and colleagues cared about his death.}
{align:justify|Among the reports was one passage that read: "Only in the past year did people begin to notice that Truong Vi Luc's bold, virile, masculine bearing had suddenly changed, and that his gestures, actions, and voice... seemed in some ways feminine. Everyone assumed... it was probably because he had grown up in a house with three elder sisters and worked constantly alongside female nurses..."}
{align:justify|When his body was examined, doctors said the female hormones in him were excessively dominant, utterly overwhelming his manhood.}
{align:justify|In the file of Doctor Ngo Uy Xuong, who had treated him, it was further noted: "Patient Truong Vi Luc cannot absorb male hormones to maintain balance, and even suffers from 'castration-like impotence' (loss of manhood)." The file went on to record that he had suffered this "castrated" condition for a very long time.}
{align:justify|The investigation showed his family background was excellent, and that he himself was pure, proud, and conservative by nature. Though he left his parents young to work on his own, living alone amid the temptations of a glittering metropolis like Bangkok, and though his workplace was surrounded by beautiful women, he kept himself as spotless as jade — truly rare and admirable.}
{align:justify|Another valuable research report stated: "Truong Vi Luc was from southern Thailand. He passed the entrance examination to the Bangkok Medical University with honors, and during all six years of medical school he lived in a rented house near campus. Every day for breakfast he liked coffee and fruit juice."}
{align:justify|Most important of all: "At noon and in the evening he always ate chicken rice — the famous Hainan chicken rice that Thai people love so much. No green vegetables, just chicken with rice: a quick meal, convenient and tasty. Truong Vi Luc was especially fond of wings and heads, and he ate them steadily for six years"...}
{align:justify|An even more valuable finding: "In the past, farm-raised chickens took at least six or seven months before they were sold. But now, thanks to scientific farming, it takes only 40 days for a chicken to grow to full market weight. In olden days, a rooster raised to adolescence could not grow fat and plump, so to make it tastier people castrated it, making the flesh rich and fatty. With modern progress, science has invented drugs that rob chickens and other poultry of their reproductive capacity. Such drugs are made into pellets and implanted in the comb or the wing muscle; they are also mixed into the feed."}
{align:justify|So while we savor fattened capon meat, little do we know that we are swallowing those very "castrating" drugs into our own bellies — no wonder so many men today suffer "castration-like impotence"! It all comes from eating capons and gelded pigs.}
{align:justify|"Truong Vi Luc was especially fond of chicken wings and heads — yet those very parts are where the drugs concentrate. The first harm this medicine does is to suppress all male hormones; that 'castrating' drug seeped into Truong Vi Luc's body continuously for six straight years. These chemicals silently accumulated and took effect until they amounted to a horrifying, massive dose. In the end, even Doctor Truong Vi Luc could not cure his own 'castrated' condition."}
{align:justify|What joy is left in being alive, then? That was the preliminary conclusion about the cause of Doctor Truong's suicide. It happened, of course, without his knowing — but based on the Truong family background, the investigators obtained one more shocking report:}
{align:justify|It turned out that Truong Vi Luc's grandfather had spent his whole life castrating pigs and chickens back in their home village. His father had even followed the old man at the trade for two or three years before, unwilling to inherit his father's "castrating" profession, he abandoned it. Who could have known that the act of inflicting extinction on other creatures would bring calamity upon the third generation, driving the grandson to suicide and leaving the line without issue? Who would still dare disbelieve that this is karmic retribution?}

{align:justify|In the great triangular delta of the Mekong River in the Kingdom of Thailand, the soil is fertile and the rains abundant — a land rich in rice production. Rice mills there are therefore enormous in scale, veritable industries in their own right.}
{align:justify|Mr. Luong is an ethnic Chinese, third-generation descendant of the Luong clan. He lives not far from famed Bangkok and runs a rice-milling business.}
{align:justify|Mr. Luong has just spent over a hundred thousand to hold a funeral for a dog — and the story, told in full, is deeply moving.}
{align:justify|Today is the day merit comes to fullness — the seventh-day rites for the dog. This evening the cremation will be held. Few guests have come; before the cremation Mr. Luong has invited a renowned senior monk to chant the sutras, and the whole ceremony is filmed and recorded.}
{align:justify|The host has even prepared a beautiful commemorative book for each guest. On the cover is printed the image of a valiant Thai dog standing beside a three-year-old child.}
{align:justify|The story began four years ago, not long after Mr. Luong married. One day he and his wife rode by boat to a temple to make offerings and create merit. After the vegetarian feast that day, as they boarded the boat, they spotted a dog fallen into the river, gasping its last. Husband and wife hastily pulled the dog aboard, dried it off, and fearing it would catch cold, wrapped it in a warm towel and brought it home. Both were fond of this female dog.}
{align:justify|The next year Mrs. Luong gave birth to a baby boy — and just then the female dog gave birth to a male pup. A folk saying goes: "A single pig means poverty; a single dog means wealth."}
{align:justify|Meaning that giving birth to one dog will help its owner prosper with good fortune. And indeed, before long good luck came to the house. As the government wanted the countryside to develop, it built a great highway right past Mr. Luong's mill. Land prices along the boulevard soared overnight — and luckily Mr. Luong's ancestors had left him a great deal of land beside the road. The property market boomed and prices climbed beyond all expectation. Moreover, once the highway was finished, transport became far more convenient, and the rice mill flourished as if the wind itself filled its sails. With fortune arriving so suddenly, everyone credited the little puppy. Mr. Luong named it "Good Luck" and called his own little boy Tieu Ba.}
{align:justify|Good Luck grew day by day, the closest of companions to the little master of the same age. When Tieu Ba toddled about learning to walk, Good Luck patiently stayed at his side to support him, inseparable as shadow and form. Mr. Luong said it was just like a nanny.}
{align:justify|One dusky evening, the mill workers got into a mass brawl with workers from another mill. Nobody paid any attention to the little young master. The three-year-old, fond of running everywhere, wandered off and fell into the river. Seeing Tieu Ba thrashing, sinking in the blink of an eye, Good Luck was alarmed; sparing nothing, it threw itself into the river and hoisted its little master up on its back. Tieu Ba clung tightly to the dog. But the riverbank was high and steep — impossible to climb. Good Luck's four legs paddled ceaselessly, straining to hold its little master up. Since Tieu Ba gripped its head fast, it could only lift its nose skyward now and then to breathe. When Mr. Luong and his wife discovered that the young maid hired to watch Tieu Ba was craning her neck at the brawl instead, they demanded urgently:}
{align:justify|"Where is the young master?"}
{align:justify|Everyone scattered to search, and just in time they found Tieu Ba and Good Luck in the river.}
{align:justify|Mr. Luong jumped straight into the water, lifted Tieu Ba up first, then hauled Good Luck out. By then Good Luck's strength was spent and it was sinking; Mr. Luong strained with all his might to rescue his beloved dog and carry it home.}
{align:justify|His son had escaped danger, and Good Luck gradually revived.}
{align:justify|Good Luck had risked its life to save its little master. This noble deed earned it not only its owners' redoubled love but the admiration and praise of all the neighbors. Yet Good Luck was not like a human — it did not grow proud from favor. It remained as before, always close beside its little master.}
{align:justify|Just as everyone was warmly discussing the dog's rescue of its little master, something unthinkable happened.}
{align:justify|It turned out the maid who watched Tieu Ba was still young and playful, so her master sent her off to scrub the toilets and temporarily entrusted the nursemaid's duties to an older, steadier woman.}
{align:justify|Three months passed. One afternoon the nurse took Tieu Ba and Good Luck to play in the vegetable garden beside the mill. Suddenly a spectacled cobra appeared, its body as thick as an arm and over two meters long, its head rearing up to strike at the nurse, biting her on the neck. The snake was brooding over its eggs, and animal mothers in the nursing season are fiercely aggressive, ever ready to attack — it is the heaven-given nature of motherhood protecting its young.}
{align:justify|Having bitten the nurse, the mother snake turned on Tieu Ba and began its attack. Clever Good Luck, standing beside Tieu Ba, saw the danger at once and leapt to stand between them — precisely as the snake darted in to strike — and took the bite in his place. Though wounded, the dog still managed to sink its teeth into the venomous snake's tail, and the two thrashed fiercely on the ground.}
{align:justify|In that moment the nurse could cry out only once — "Help!" — before the venom spread and she collapsed.}
{align:justify|Little Tieu Ba, innocent and unafraid, just stood there watching the snake and the dog fight. When the workers and Mr. Luong arrived, the nurse's face had already turned black and she could no longer speak. Good Luck too had venom running to its heart; blood streamed from its eyes and all its strength was gone. But it still clenched the snake's tail fast and would not let go.}
{align:justify|The people beat the venomous snake to death, but Good Luck was too deeply poisoned. It gazed at its master one last time and breathed its last.}
{align:justify|Mr. Luong's tears fell like rain. He just held his beloved dog in his arms. The others roused Mr. Luong, reminding him the dog's body carried venom. In that moment of parting, Mr. Luong chokingly instructed that the loyal and righteous Good Luck be given funeral rites of the utmost solemnity.}
{align:justify|And that funeral was held for seven days and seven nights — the grandest ever held for a dog.}
{align:justify|When the rites were complete, all were moved by the loyal heart of the dog. Everyone praised the fact that, because Mr. and Mrs. Luong had rescued the dog four years before, they now reaped this good retribution.}
{align:justify|It shows that humans and animals — like this dog — can live together in harmony as companions, rescuing and sheltering one another, close as limbs of one body.}
{align:justify|Therefore people should never mistreat dogs, much less brutally kill and eat them.}

{align:justify|Fishing is an exceedingly cruel pastime. Just imagine driving that sharp hook into your own lips or tongue — the pain would pierce to heart and lungs. No one could possibly bear it.}
{align:justify|Yet many people take fishing as a hobby and fish tirelessly. Some have even founded fishing clubs. The claim that "fishing trains the character" is nonsense — the talk of people without an ounce of compassion.}
{align:justify|Lam Giap Xuan was a Thai government official with a great passion for fishing and a member of the fishing club. On weekend holidays he would often take a boat out to sea to fish, sipping wine with tasty morsels while he waited — bliss beyond words. Everyone drank fine wine, ate fresh fish, and savored earthly pleasures to while away the hours.}
{align:justify|A few years back, when the club was newly founded, the atmosphere was lively and bustling. But since last year, membership has shriveled from 30 down to just 7 or 8.}
{align:justify|The club now hangs heavy with a deathly air; everyone is dispirited and no longer cares to go sea-fishing — and though Lam Giap Xuan is a stubborn man who does not believe in karmic retribution, a man with no family and no faith, even he cannot breathe life back into the club or rouse anyone to sail out fishing as before. The reason is two uncanny events, one after another.}
{align:justify|The first happened to old member A Ban himself. That Sunday, A Ban and his wife traveled inland to wish his mother-in-law a happy birthday. His mother-in-law was a person of standing and local prestige, and birthday guests packed the house. The feast was lavish — beef, pork, fish, chicken, duck, goose... whatever one could wish for. The guests feasted to their hearts' content and still could not finish it all.}
{align:justify|A Ban loved fish and innards; seeing a big fish on the banquet table, he was delighted. Eating carelessly, chewing poorly, and gulping down too many fish innards at once, he choked the moment he swallowed. Feeling something hard stuck in his throat, he frantically tried to pull it out with his fingers — but nothing could dislodge it. In that moment of being unable to swallow or pull it out, he could hardly breathe, and could not even open his mouth to cry for help.}
{align:justify|His younger brother, sitting opposite, was the first to notice the crisis and rushed over to help — but A Ban's eyes had already rolled back, his head slumped, and his breathing faded. Everyone rushed him to the emergency room, but halfway there he breathed his last.}
{align:justify|Though he was dead, the hospital still operated to determine the cause of death. The doctor pulled the fish innards lodged in his throat...}
{align:justify|And at that very instant, every doctor, nurse, and orderly present gasped in horror, eyes frozen and mouths stiff: the murderer was... a fish hook, lodged fast in A Ban's throat. Strangely, the hook had been hidden inside the fish's innards; once down A Ban's gullet it was squeezed outward, so the harder A Ban pulled, the tighter the hook gripped. Friends and relatives who witnessed this dreadful sight naturally thought at once of A Ban's beloved hobby of fishing — and his experience and skill had commanded the admiration of every club member. When others caught nothing, he hauled in plenty.}
{align:justify|Now, gazing at A Ban's rolled-back eyes and gaping mouth, beside the blood-covered hook, everyone's hair stood on end. No one could disbelieve that karmic retribution follows like shadow after form — what happened to A Ban was a thousand times, ten thousand times true!}
{align:justify|The second story is even stranger. Member Duong Ty Ich was a master angler who had entered tournaments and twice in a row taken the championship.}
{align:justify|Besides fishing, Mr. Duong loved riding his electric bicycle. Every day at dusk he would take his wife out to the suburbs for air.}
{align:justify|That night around 11 o'clock, after attending a close friend's funeral, he drove home. The road was so familiar he could have driven it blindfolded. But something most uncanny happened: while the car was running smoothly, he suddenly saw a great river appear before him — one he had never seen there before. To avoid plunging in, at 120 kilometers per hour he swerved hard and slammed the brakes. There was a tremendous CRASH — his car hit a power pole by the road. When he came to, he was lying in hospital.}
{align:justify|Everyone assumed Mr. Duong had crashed from speeding. That would have been an ordinary accident — not strange enough. What was strangest was that the wounds on his body healed quickly, except for his lips and palate, which were gravely injured. All his teeth had fallen out and he could eat nothing; for over a month food could only be pumped in liquid form through a tube into his throat. Stranger still were the tears above and below the corners of his mouth — doctors stitched them seven times and still they would not close.}
{align:justify|For every time they were stitched, when the sutures were removed the corners of his mouth festered and swelled again. Finally the doctors used the most advanced chemical sutures to sew them shut. A week later the stitches dissolved by themselves — but his lips were still ulcerated, both corners gaping wide like a fish's jaws. The attending physician had to admit defeat. For months he was racked with pain and endless misery, his mouth ulcerated and stretched wide, exactly like a fish's mouth torn and ulcerated by a hook.}
{align:justify|One day his wife came to visit as usual and blurted out without thinking: "Goodness! Your mouth gapes like that — just like a hooked fish's!"}
{align:justify|Those words struck Mr. Duong's conscience like a warning bell. He remembered how every time he caught a fish, he tore its mouth and ulcerated its whole palate. Only now did he truly feel its pain deep in his bones. So husband and wife hastened to the temple with incense and lamps; before the Buddha he bowed most sincerely in repentance and vowed never to fish again.}
{align:justify|Strange to tell, from that day on his lips gradually healed and were no longer ulcerated or swollen. Just a week later he was discharged. Afterward he told his fellow club members all he had been through and urged them to fish no more.}
{align:justify|From then on, a great many of the fishing club's members retired — a drastic decline. Before long Lam Xuan Giap too gave up fishing, and the club disbanded.}

{align:justify|In a rural region of eastern Thailand there was a farmer named A Bang, whose wife had borne him three children — two boys and a girl. The eldest, O, was 9; the second, Hong, was 7; and the youngest girl, Bach, was 5.}
{align:justify|One day, A Bang and his wife went to a ceremony at a relative's house, leaving the three little ones at home. They lived in a wooden house built in the Thai style. Beside it stood a stack of dry straw, along with a cross-shaped gutter for catching rainwater and a row of jars for drinking water.}
{align:justify|The farmhouses here stand very far apart, scattered across the fields.}
{align:justify|That day the wooden house suddenly caught fire. Distant neighbors could only see black smoke towering up and flames engulfing the house; the whole wooden building was swiftly consumed. The three children inside could flee nowhere, for the gate was locked fast.}
{align:justify|Thinking that water conquers fire, little O carried his siblings one by one and put them into two big jars full of water, covering the lids. Then he too jumped into another jar to escape the flames.}
{align:justify|When neighbors and firefighters arrived, the wooden house and four straw stacks had burned to nothing, along with a tethered ox — but no trace of the children was found. Everyone assumed they must have escaped. When A Bang and his wife returned, they too searched frantically for their children, hunting until nightfall without finding them.}
{align:justify|The next morning, a relative named Luan Bao came to help search. As if forewarned, he rushed straight to the water jars and lifted a lid — then cried out in shock. Hearing his cry, A Bang and his wife ran over to look: there was the body of O, their eldest son. Luan Bao opened the other jars in turn and found, one after another, the bodies of Hong and Bach. At this the couple collapsed in grief and fainted away.}
{align:justify|Newspapers and television all carried the heartbreaking news. A TV reporter interviewed Luan Bao, asking how he had known the three children had died in the jars.}
{align:justify|Luan Bao answered: "The night the fire broke out, I was asleep at home when I dreamed that everyone was running about searching for the children — and suddenly an unfamiliar old man with a beard white down to his chest told me: 'The school of fish is in the water jars!'"}
{align:justify|Waking, I found it most strange, so I hurried over to A Bang's house and rushed to lift the jar lids — never imagining the children's bodies were inside...}
{align:justify|Hearing this, A Bang gave a great cry and fainted dead away. When revived, his eyes were vacant as he stared blankly at his three children's corpses, muttering:}
{align:justify|"Retribution! It is retribution!"}
{align:justify|Everyone thought grief had unhinged his mind and dared ask nothing.}
{align:justify|As is the custom in the countryside, when a family suffers, all join hearts and hands to help bury A Bang's children.}
{align:justify|Within a single day, A Bang and his wife lost all three beloved children and had to shelter elsewhere. All their accumulated property had fed the flames. Facing this tragic upheaval, they tasted deeply the impermanence of human joy and sorrow, parting and reunion — and without hesitation they asked together to renounce the world and enter monastic life.}
{align:justify|All understood and sympathized. But everyone wondered at hearing A Bang constantly mutter, "Retribution! Retribution!"... He had always been an honest, hardworking farmer — what could he have done to deserve such heavy punishment? It truly baffled people.}
{align:justify|Only A Vu, A Bang's brother-in-law, knew the truth. A Vu said:}
{align:justify|"Brother Bang always kept harmony and warmly helped others. He had just one trait: he loved tasty food and invented his own dish called 'vegetables stuffed with fish' — something I protested fiercely many times.}
{align:justify|Thailand is a land of rice and fish. In spring, whenever thunderstorms come, it is spawning season. A dozen days later the fry are as big as chopstick tips, and A Bang loved to sew mosquito netting into a scoop net to catch them — a dozen at least, over a hundred at most — then keep the baby fish in a bucket of water.}
{align:justify|When it was time to cook, he would pick thick hollow vegetable stalks, cut away the joints to make smooth empty tubes. Then he poured cold water into the pot, dropped in the vegetables and the fry, and lit a low simmering fire. As the cold water slowly turned warm, at first the little fish swam about innocently; as the water grew warmer, the fry darted into the hollow stalks to hide from the heat...}
{align:justify|Watching those fish cruelly killed, dying so piteously before his very eyes... A Bang and his wife felt not a shred of pity — on the contrary, they were proud and pleased with themselves for creating such a special, delicious dish. Every hollow stalk held a school of fish inside; tossed with seasoning, it tasted incomparably delicious.}
{align:justify|Once, while working the fields, A Bang scooped up a whole school of fry with his net — when suddenly two big fish, their parents, leapt up onto the bank. Perhaps seeing their young carried off, they were so stricken that they jumped up to kill themselves in protest. But A Bang, his humanity clouded, took this windfall for granted and carried the two big fish home to cook."}
{align:justify|People grow used to creating evil killing karma because their conscience lies buried and deluded. Only when their own parents, spouses, siblings, or children die do they wail and weep, crying out to heaven in bitter grief — never thinking that animals too have the bonds of parents and siblings, husbands and wives, children...}
{align:justify|An ancient verse says:}
{align:justify|For a hundred thousand years, within the bowl of soup,}
{align:justify|Resentment deep as the sea, hatred hard to calm.}
{align:justify|Now the wheel of karmic retribution has turned, making A Bang bear the pain of burying his children and tasting the bitterness of watching a whole brood perish together — how could this not break his spirit?}
{align:justify|An ancient poet wrote these lines to discourage killing:}
{align:justify|Who says sentient beings' lives are tiny and small,}
{align:justify|Mere bones and flesh and skin, nothing at all?}
{align:justify|I beg you: do not catch, hunt, or kill them —}
{align:justify|Their feelings differ in nothing from our own!}
{align:justify|Every day humankind keeps adding to its karma of killing and grievance. When we lie sick in hospital or on the operating table, in that very hour we might quietly ask ourselves: "In all my life, how many chickens have I eaten, how many ducks, how many pigs, how many cows... How many beings have entombed themselves in my belly?" — and "Today is the day retribution arrives." Visitors at the sickbed should reflect likewise.}
{align:justify|If humankind can question itself to the very end, compassion and repentance will arise, the karma of killing will naturally lessen, and strange grievous illnesses will gradually melt away.}

{align:justify|Mr. Thai is 67 years old this year, a man of Chaozhou origin. In 1941, when Japan invaded China and his homeland was besieged and life grew harsh, he left home in his youth to earn a living in Thailand. He now lives in central Thailand, making his living by trade. For nine years his legs have been diseased — like an elephant's legs.}
{align:justify|Mr. Thai loved eating goose meat and drinking wine; every evening he consumed a platter of goose and half a jar of wine. Every single day, without fail.}
{align:justify|Ten years ago he joined a tour to northeastern China, where he tasted the world-famous bear's paw — a memory he carried home with longing. Sadly it was only that once, for back in Thailand he never had another chance to savor that precious dish. Even so, Mr. Thai kept his love of goose feet, for their flavor was somewhat similar. From then on he would often drink wine with goose feet to ease his craving for bear's paw.}
{align:justify|Later someone taught him a way of preparing goose feet, claiming it would taste no worse than bear's paw and could even enrich the essence, strengthen the breath, and nourish the spirit — a secret method of ancient kings.}
{align:justify|And so Mr. Thai learned to prepare goose feet. On an iron table ringed all around with a railing, he set a live goose to stand. Then he lit firewood beneath and heated it gradually. As the temperature rose, the iron table slowly grew hot, and the goose, unable to bear it, tucked up one foot. As the heat climbed higher, neither foot could endure it — the bird alternately tucked and released them, as if dancing. It could not flee, for all sides were fenced. When the iron table glowed red-hot, both of the goose's feet hopped frantically — like a raging tempest. It danced for its very life, nothing but dancing and dancing! Then it went wild, charging blindly, its neck stretched to the utmost, uttering pitiful, mournful cries before collapsing. Now its two feet were burned red and swollen — but it was not yet dead. Seeing this, Mr. Thai swiftly chopped the feet off. The goose fainted from pain, revived, then lapsed back into a swoon and died. But by then Mr. Thai cared nothing for that; he only took the goose feet out, washed them clean, and simmered them low with medicinal herbs.}
{align:justify|According to legend, when the goose has "danced" upon the fiery table, all the breath and blood of its body gather down into its feet, while the rest of its flesh turns tasteless, without nourishing value. The iron table's roasting drives the blood of the whole body down to concentrate in the feet — this is called "goose feet refined into elixir," and Mr. Thai ate such "elixir goose feet" for years on end.}
{align:justify|Afterward his legs fell ill — red and swollen exactly like an elephant's. Every joint in his body ached. He saw doctor after doctor and swallowed every Eastern and Western medicine, all to no avail. His legs hurt 24 hours a day, 365 days a year; this torment racked him for six long years. The misery was unbearable. Many times he wanted to kill himself, and only his good wife's restraint and his filial children's comfort kept him dragging out his remaining breath.}
{align:justify|One night he dreamed a flock of footless geese came seeking him, savagely pecking and biting. Waking drenched in sweat, he connected it at once with his sickness — surely tied to his eating of goose feet. For he had locked living geese on an iron table to roast them, watching them "dance" piteously upon the flames. The more he thought, the more his heart pounded and his flesh trembled. Involuntarily he looked down at his own red swollen legs: alas! They looked exactly like goose feet fresh from dancing on the fire before being chopped off!}
{align:justify|It was craving goose feet that had sown this evil cause, and now the wronged spirits of the geese came demanding their debt. Retribution! Retribution! Who would have thought cause and effect could ripen so swiftly? Now conscience awakened: helped by his wife to the front gate, he knelt and repented most sincerely toward high Heaven. He vowed: "From this day I will not kill, I will eat no meat — I vow to keep a clean mouth, to eat vegetarian for life, until death!" His wife beside him vowed to eat vegetarian with him.}
{align:justify|Strange to tell, from the moment he repented toward Heaven and made his vow, his legs no longer swelled and ached. Only their outward shape remained unsightly, and movement stayed difficult. To this day he takes no medicine and seeks no doctor, for he understands this is proof that karmic creditors have come to claim their debt — so why bother with treatment? He willingly keeps these "elephant" legs as evidence, to warn those who come after.}
{align:justify|Do not kill for a moment's pleasure of the palate. Know that food turns to waste the moment it passes the throat — but if you create evil karma, its retribution will pursue you without end.}
{align:justify|As the sages of old said:}
{align:justify|Misfortune and blessing have no gate — we summon them ourselves. The retribution of good and evil follows like shadow after form! If he can, Mr. Thai should do many good deeds and build merit and virtue — for only merit can dissolve grievances, untie karmic knots, and, with karma ended, end the sickness.}

{align:justify|Mr. Ngo is an ethnic Chinese in Thailand, a dealer in local produce. With little capital, he could only buy and sell at once, so his profits were slim.}
{align:justify|Recently someone introduced him to dealing in skinks, where profits run very high. Yet Mr. Ngo worked at it only three months before refusing to continue.}
{align:justify|His friends all regretted it for him: meeting such profitable work and not carrying on, content instead to scrape by on small-margin goods like onions and garlic...}
{align:justify|But Mr. Ngo felt no regret — and surely there was a reason.}
{align:justify|Why is dealing in skinks so profitable? That story starts with "Highly Tonic Gecko Wine."}
{align:justify|In the herbal compendium of the ancient physician Li Shizhen it is written: "Geckos tonify the lungs, boost the breath, strengthen the kidneys, enrich essence and blood, settle asthma, and stop coughs."}
{align:justify|Eastern medicine treats phlegm-asthma chiefly with gecko, which is why tonic gecko wine became such an easy seller on the market.}
{align:justify|Geckos were bought, compounded with medicine, and steeped in wine; demand kept growing, and geckos were hunted nearly to extinction. So the pharmacists substituted skinks for geckos. Now in China skinks too have been caught nearly to extinction. So the pharmacies turned to buying skinks in Thailand. Farmers in northeastern Thailand, in their spare season, band together to catch skinks for extra income.}
{align:justify|Some have even abandoned farming to grow rich catching skinks. The To Vuong family is the classic example.}
{align:justify|The To Vuongs — husband, wife, and son, three mouths in all — were once very poor and had to rent paddies to farm. But since switching to catching skinks, within just three short years they owned a dozen-odd acres of land and even bought a small truck. With profits so rich, the villagers all coveted them, and the skink-catching craze surged like a tide.}
{align:justify|Yet a farmer, catching all day, could take only a dozen-odd skinks at most — while To Vuong's wealth was plain to see, with money even in the bank. For besides catching skinks himself,}
{align:justify|To Vuong also bought up live skinks the villagers caught, brought them home to dry, and only then sold them on. The skinks that died at the To Vuong family's hands numbered in the hundreds of thousands.}
{align:justify|Two things drove Mr. Ngo to abandon the skink trade.}
{align:justify|The first: one day Mr. Ngo happened to pass the To Vuong house and witnessed them making dried skinks.}
{align:justify|The live skinks they had caught and bought were brought home. Then with a knife they slit the bellies and scooped out the innards — yet at this point the skinks were still not dead. To Vuong then took a sharpened bamboo skewer and drove it through the mouth straight to the tail, leaving the creatures writhing endlessly, eyes wide open, staring at the people. Only then did To Vuong lay the skinks out to dry in the sun. Witnessing this cruel scene, Mr. Ngo shuddered and turned straight away, not daring to look back.}
{align:justify|The second: in March of a certain year, during Thailand's annual nationwide water-splashing festival, Mr. Ngo was traveling from Chiang Mai back to Bangkok when, crossing a mountain pass, he witnessed a tragic accident. A small truck had overturned into the deep ravine beside the road. Three people lay dead at the scene — the To Vuong family: husband, wife, and son.}
{align:justify|That day, though it was the water festival, To Vuong had not rested but kept driving out to buy skinks — and the whole family met disaster on the mountain pass, their truck flipping into the ravine. To Vuong's wife and child were thrown from the vehicle, their bodies mangled. In death their eyes still bulged wide open — exactly like the eyes of skinks in death. More horrifying still, beyond looking, was To Vuong himself: shot from the cab swift as a bullet, neither veering left nor right, he landed precisely upon a sharpened bamboo stake the road-menders had just cut on the ravine's edge below. To Vuong was pierced straight through from mouth to anus — exactly as To Vuong had done to the skinks.}
{align:justify|Mr. Ngo witnessed with his own eyes the appalling deaths of the whole To Vuong family. Gooseflesh rippled over him, and all he could blurt out was: "Retribution! It is truly retribution!"}
{align:justify|From then on he abandoned entirely every trade connected with buying and selling animals.}

{align:justify|There was a district hospital in the highlands of Thailand where, transport being inconvenient, patients had to come from early morning for examinations and hurry home before dark.}
{align:justify|One Sunday, all the patients needing examination had been seen, so the doctors looked forward to a free afternoon. Suddenly a nurse rushed to report to the Director: "Someone has a fishbone stuck in his throat and needs emergency rescue — please come at once!"}
{align:justify|It was a young man over twenty. Breathing had ceased, though the body was still warm; his pallid face showed utter agony and terror. According to his family and friends, he had just breathed his last as he was carried in. Though the patient was dead, hospital rules required an autopsy to establish the cause of death and issue a death certificate. The doctors then listened as the family recounted the fatal chain of events.}
{align:justify|The young man who died of the stuck fish was named Thinh. Early that morning he had gone out to the fields with several young friends to catch fish. Heavy rain the night before had flooded paddies, ponds, and ditches; fish and shrimp were everywhere, and everyone caught a great many. Thinh caught a mountain-crucian, then spotted a big carp ahead of him. Overjoyed, he hurried over to grab the carp — but his hand was holding the crucian with nowhere safe to put it, and he could not let it go. In his haste he decided to hold the fish in his mouth. To his shock, the little fish slipped straight in and "dug in" at his throat. Feeling sharp pain, Thinh tried to hook it out with his fingers — but too late: the whole fish lay in his gullet and could not be pulled out.}
{align:justify|This mountain-crucian is small and very common in Thai rice fields — plump-bodied, about two to three inches long, its golden scales glittering yet very hard and sharp. Stroked with the grain it feels smooth, but pulled backward the scales become hooks that grip fast.}
{align:justify|So the harder Thinh dragged at the fish, the more those "hooks" did their deadly work. The species is remarkably tough and hardy — it can leave the water and crawl right up a mountain peak, which is why it is called the mountain-crucian.}
{align:justify|Within minutes Thinh began struggling to breathe. The stubborn fish sat lodged in his throat — impossible to swallow, impossible to spit out, impossible even to scream. Thinh could only point at his mouth, eyes rolling back.}
{align:justify|Seeing this, his friends panicked and pried Thinh's mouth open to get the fish out — in vain. They could only stare as Thinh writhed in agony.}
{align:justify|One friend quickly called a taxi to rush Thinh to hospital, but halfway there he breathed his last.}
{align:justify|By now the hospital was operating. The doctor cut open Thinh's throat and took out the mountain-crucian — still not dead. A nurse tossed it into the pond before the hospital, joking:}
{align:justify|"If the police investigate, they'll be hunting the culprit — off you go and hide in the pond!"}
{align:justify|Thinh's body was claimed by his family and brought home.}
{align:justify|Thinh having died with a live fish stuck in him, the family held his funeral. By Thai custom, the dead are cremated after seven days.}
{align:justify|Thinh's sudden young death grieved many. But that very morning, even as Thinh's cremation was being held, came the astonishing news that someone else had been killed by a mountain-crucian — and more astonishing still, the dead man was Mr. Thong, Thinh's father, with a mountain-crucian stuck in his throat in exactly the same way: unable to swallow or spit it out, unable to breathe, and dead. Precisely the manner of Thinh's death.}
{align:justify|This was truly the strangest event of the day, shaking the whole district and province.}
{align:justify|For the cremation was held toward evening that day, with all friends and relatives attending. Mr. Thong, Thinh's father, wanting to prepare a feast for the guests, had gone out at dawn to catch fish in the ditch. To catch the fish easily, the water first had to be partly bailed out. Then big and little fish, crowded together, thrashed and jostled ceaselessly, and he simply scooped them up with a basket — catching fish while calling loudly for his wife to bring a bucket...}
{align:justify|Slow to tell, but it happened in a flash: just as Mr. Thong opened his mouth wide to call his wife, a mountain-crucian leapt high — neither veering left nor right — and dropped straight into his open mouth. Taken by utter surprise and panic, Mr. Thong strained with all his might to spit and cough it out and hooked at it with his fingers... but the tough, hardy mountain-crucian had darted straight down his gullet, settled there, and would go no further.}
{align:justify|Mr. Thong was carried up onto the ditch bank, eyes already rolled back, and breathed his last on the way to hospital.}
{align:justify|This was truly a one-of-a-kind wonder, rumored through the whole village, the whole district, then the whole province.}
{align:justify|Within one short week the hospital had handled the deaths of both father and son of the Thong family.}
{align:justify|The affair was sensational and baffling. So the Director visited the bereaved family to offer condolences and took pains to find out why father and son had both been killed by live fish within seven days.}
{align:justify|It turned out the Thong family had lived there for generations. Whenever the harvest allowed, they caught and raised fish as a side trade, and the household had grown rich on it.}
{align:justify|Mr. Thong caught fish, dried them, and sold them: while the fish were still alive, he drove a bamboo skewer from each fish's mouth down its throat, five to ten fish per skewer, then smoke-dried or sun-dried them. Each string of fish was hung on a bamboo frame awaiting sale.}
{align:justify|From his forefathers down to Thinh, seven generations had done just this.}
{align:justify|Villagers watching Mr. Thong drive sharp bamboo down the throats of living fish, leaving them writhing in pain, found the cruelty more than eyes could bear.}
{align:justify|No one in a million could have imagined that father and son would be killed by live fish in exactly the same manner.}
{align:justify|Set side by side — "people hook fish, fish hook people" — here is the uncanny working of retribution. As Buddhism says: "Karmic retribution never wrongs the innocent" — cause and effect turns with perfect fairness. Let all be warned: never, ever sow evil causes.}
{align:justify|At a certain district hospital in Thailand, the Director — a standing specialist who also wielded the scalpel himself — once told the strange tale of one operation, waking listeners to aspire toward goodness.}
{align:justify|The Director said: "In all my years as a doctor, I have never seen, nor ever treated, so bizarre an illness. The sufferer was operated on five times in three years."}
{align:justify|And each time more grave than the last. In the end even his arms and legs had to be sawn off, leaving him disabled with one arm and one leg.}
{align:justify|The patient of this strange illness was named Van Lai. Once, a softshell turtle bit his hand and wounded his little finger. At first he came to hospital just for a hemostatic dressing, thinking it nothing — but half a month later the wounded finger joint became inflamed, swollen, and painful. Examination diagnosed poisonous bacteria eating into the bone: the little finger had to go, lest the poison spread and endanger his life. So it was cut off, leaving nine fingers.}
{align:justify|Less than half a year later, Van Lai went to the seaside — and unluckily a softshell turtle bit and wounded his little toe.}
{align:justify|Days later the foot became inflamed, swollen, and painful. Back at hospital, X-rays showed the poison had reached the bone: the little toe had to be removed to be safe.}
{align:justify|Within the year, the little finger's stump and the little toe's stump both became inflamed, swollen, and painful. Van Lai returned for examination and X-rays — alas, all was wrong: this time the poison threatened to form tumors, and hand and foot had to go. The patient consented to surgery.}
{align:justify|So many misfortunes befell Van Lai that tongues wagged at the marvel of it. Yet stranger things still came trailing after.}
{align:justify|One day, the son of an acquaintance was ordained as a monk, and Van Lai joined everyone at the head-shaving ceremony. Since ordinations in Thailand are usually held at four or five in the morning, participants sleep over at the temple for convenience.}
{align:justify|They slept together in the Buddha hall, some forty or fifty people — yet misfortune landed squarely on Van Lai.}
{align:justify|There was a rat which, among dozens of sound sleepers, picked precisely the stump of Van Lai's sawn leg to bite. Van Lai screamed and moaned in pain, startling the whole sleeping crowd. Though the rat only broke the skin and drew a speck of blood, people began to talk. They said: "Rats bite only lifeless things. If a rat bites you by chance, it proves you are but a corpse without a soul — that is why the rat dared bite him..."}
{align:justify|The chatter buzzed to the skies and left Van Lai all the more frightened and uneasy; he secretly thought he might not live much longer! Though some comforted him not to be so superstitious, he still felt his life was threatened — and the two sawn stumps of arm and leg kept aching and itching dully.}
{align:justify|So Van Lai went to hospital for examination.}
{align:justify|After a thorough check the doctor said: "It is bad — the X-rays show the old poison has spread to the bone at both wounds. As before, tumors have spread far; unless we remove the arm and the shin, there is no hope." And so they were cut away — the arm from below the elbow, the leg from below the knee.}
{align:justify|Within three years, on Van Lai alone, the surgeons had to operate five times. The Director, who had operated on him himself, suspected he must once have done something monstrous and made a special inquiry into Van Lai's history and background. At last the doctor learned the whole truth:}
{align:justify|"Van Lai, 43 years old, a farmer and part-time construction worker.}
{align:justify|Normally fond of drink and river fish, he was especially fond of turtles, softshell turtles, and the like...}
{align:justify|Van Lai had heard people say: 'Whoever eats ten to twenty softshell turtles will never suffer rheumatism or weather ailments for life, and will even nourish yin and tonify yang...' So he would feast on softshell turtle fried with chili, washed down with a flagon of white wine. He indulged his palate to the full. For twenty years he ate this way, never tiring of it.}
{align:justify|One day Van Lai bought an enormous softshell turtle at market, weighing a dozen-odd kilos. He was as happy as if he had struck gold. So rare a treasure could not be eaten up in one meal, of course — it had to be kept so it could be enjoyed gradually, without need of a freezer...}
{align:justify|So Van Lai devised a brilliant method: 'cut the turtle up and eat it gradually.' For turtles and softshell turtles live long, endure well, and are hard to kill outright. Penned up without food, one can ordinarily survive half a year.}
{align:justify|Van Lai's masterstroke of cuisine was: however much meat he wanted that day, that much he cut off. Then he smeared lime on the fresh cut to stop the bleeding. In this way the turtle could be eaten from over ten days to half a month and still be alive — and at the end he cut off its head and ate it whole.}
{align:justify|From the day Van Lai invented this 'novel' way of eating and carried it out successfully, he acted accordingly ever after — and no one knows how many softshell turtles he ate.}
{align:justify|Kind souls warned him that so cold-blooded and cruel a practice, so conscienceless, would bring evil retribution! But he cared nothing — so long as there was fresh, tasty turtle meat, enough.}
{align:justify|Not until his own body was cut and sawn away piece by piece, tasting that flavor himself, did he know that retribution in this very life was falling upon him. But regret came too late."}
{align:justify|The Director gathered his collected materials, read them carefully, and wrote this conclusion:}
{align:justify|"Medical science is powerless to explain true stories of karmic retribution like these!"}

{align:justify|In a village in southern Thailand, a young man surnamed Luu celebrated his wedding, and the bride was a girl nicknamed "Miss Monkey."}
{align:justify|At first everyone assumed "Miss Monkey" must be an extremely ugly girl, or a maiden covered in hair and shaped just like a monkey — hence the nickname.}
{align:justify|But when the newlyweds finished bowing to Heaven and Earth and came out arm in arm to greet the guests, everyone's eyes lit up, and all cried out as one from the bottom of their hearts:}
{align:justify|"Heavens! The bride is beautiful as a fairy!..."}
{align:justify|Yet all were burning with curiosity about the name "Miss Monkey" and longed to inquire into her story.}
{align:justify|It turned out that "Miss Monkey's" mother had in her youth been a celebrated beauty, with countless young men at her feet. Admirers from all around came hoping for one glimpse of her face; many earnestly sought her hand. Even a rich young man from neighboring Malaysia crossed the border to propose. But the beauty gracefully declined, saying she did not wish to marry a foreigner and leave her parents.}
{align:justify|Before long the beauty married her childhood sweetheart from the same village. He was handsome and gifted, she was radiant and lovely — truly a match made in Heaven. All the young men and women around envied them.}
{align:justify|Soon the bride was with child, and her husband cared for her more tenderly than ever, providing for her every need. Her parents-in-law were all delighted and showered her with kindness.}
{align:justify|Normally she ate easily, but with pregnancy it was the reverse: however delicious the food, she could not swallow a bite.}
{align:justify|Her husband racked his brains a hundred ways to tempt his beloved wife's appetite.}
{align:justify|One day he bought a monkey's foot at market, stewed it low with northern medicinal herbs like yam, ginseng, angelica, and the like, then set it before her by surprise — and at last aroused her craving. Seeing his wife eat heartily, he was overjoyed.}
{align:justify|From then on, every market day he bought home a monkey's foot or a piece of monkey meat for his wife.}
{align:justify|One day at market he met a hunter selling a bound monkey. Delighted, the husband bought it and brought it home, thinking to keep it a few days before killing it for his wife to eat.}
{align:justify|That day his wife was seven months pregnant, sitting in an armchair watching her husband kill the monkey.}
{align:justify|The pitiful monkey had for days been fed nothing but bananas and fruit; the fat on its body amounted to less than half a pound, the rest to serve as tonic for the expectant mother.}
{align:justify|Seeing the husband take up a knife to kill it, the monkey's eyes streamed with tears and it knelt on its legs like a human, begging its master to spare its life.}
{align:justify|But this wife-doting husband felt not a stir, not a spark of mercy. Raising the blade, he aimed at its heart.}
{align:justify|At the brink of death, the monkey swiftly seized the handle with its right hand and gripped the blade tight with its left, resisting death with all its strength, refusing to let go. The husband strained but could not drive the knife in. Thinking to draw the knife back and stab again, in that very motion of withdrawal — because the monkey's four fingers gripped so tightly — two segments of its fingers were sliced clean off, leaving only the last joints of the four fingers, counting inward from the knuckle. The expectant mother saw blood gushing like a stream; her cruel resolve melted, and she cried out again and again for her husband to stop: "Don't kill it! Kill no more! Set the monkey free at once!"}
{align:justify|Through relentless struggle the monkey snatched its life back from the gates of hell. Enduring the pain, it bolted for the door — and before leaving the gate it turned back to look at the couple, its eyes full of hatred, then vanished without a trace.}
{align:justify|From then on, the wife vomited at the smell of meat and ate only vegetables and tofu. Her family, fearing she lacked nourishment, secretly mixed meat broth into her food — but one taste made her retch violently.}
{align:justify|When her time came, she bore a baby girl whose features were perfectly formed — except that on her left hand there was only a thumb and the last joints of four fingers, shaped exactly as if sliced by a sharp blade. Parents and relatives sighed at the sight, murmuring, "How strange!"}
{align:justify|Thailand is Buddhist and believes deeply in cause and effect; all said as one: "This is karmic retribution, come from the husband's severing of the monkey's four fingers."}
{align:justify|Today the bride "Miss Monkey" wears a white wedding gown and white gloves, gentle and lovely as a fairy descended to earth.}
{align:justify|Yet who would know that inside those gloves there are no four long slender fingers — only four remaining stumps, as if chopped off? That is the origin of the nickname "Miss Monkey," a story on everyone's lips in those days.}

{align:justify|In construction circles today there are three famous sayings:}
{align:justify|Skimp on labor but not on materials: wait for collapse!}
{align:justify|Skimp on materials but not on labor: it will collapse!}
{align:justify|Skimp on both labor and materials: it will certainly collapse!}
{align:justify|In short: "cutting corners and skimping materials" means collapse sooner or later!}
{align:justify|In 1971, in Tainan City, Taiwan, there was a well-known contractor named Truong Si Luong. Unschooled in books, he nevertheless built houses of great sturdiness. There was never any cutting of corners or skimping of materials; he always guarded his reputation and honor. So for private homes and government works alike, everyone wanted him as contractor.}
{align:justify|Truong Si Luong had two sons. The elder, Truong Kim Sung, was a trained design engineer. The younger, Truong Tat Khoan, inherited his father's business.}
{align:justify|At that time the government wanted to build an apartment block in the countryside — a hugely expensive project — and shareholders of both the underworld and the establishment fought to win the contract. Because Truong Si Luong enjoyed a good name, local government officials all recommended him and invited him to take charge of the construction.}
{align:justify|At the county government there was an official named Chiem Tuu Thanh, risen by hard study, who had passed the civil service examination with excellent results. Pity only that he had no self-respect and kept himself unclean. Young in years, he had early learned every evil habit: deceit, bribe-taking, framing the innocent, glib talk masking rotten work, and the like...}
{align:justify|Learning that the eldest son Truong Kim Sung had signed the design contract, he slipped into Kim Sung's house at midnight, bringing along several councilors and triad brothers, falsely claiming the project had been won through many people's influence and muscle — and that now that Mr. Sung had smoothly signed the contract, he must share a bowl of rice with them, what they called an "introduction fee."}
{align:justify|Seeing Mr. Thanh's menacing gang and fearing for his life, Kim Sung had to compromise and agree.}
{align:justify|Next Mr. Thanh led his people to the site manager, the younger son Truong Tat Khoan. Besides demanding a "guarantee fee," Thanh claimed subcontracting rights, naming which construction firms must be used — or else there would be trouble.}
{align:justify|Now this project was never very profitable, and after layer upon layer of threats and extortion,}
{align:justify|Tat Khoan, forced into a cooperation he could not describe, gritted his teeth and let Tuu Thanh take control. And so Tuu Thanh freely manipulated the skimping of labor and materials, cleverly bribing the inspectors to obtain approvals with ease.}
{align:justify|Tuu Thanh believed his methods watertight — no one could ever trace a thing.}
{align:justify|But man proposes and Heaven disposes. The year after the apartment block was completed, Tainan suffered grave floods and typhoons. When the floods passed, the block could not withstand the great storm winds: in a single afternoon the high-rise suddenly collapsed, bricks and tiles flying in all directions.}
{align:justify|And this calamity unfolded precisely as senior officials were touring to survey the disaster — they witnessed it all with their own eyes and ordered their subordinates to investigate.}
{align:justify|The crafty Chiem Tuu Thanh at once dumped all blame on Truong Kim Sung and Truong Tat Khoan. The shrewd Kim Sung, knowing responsibility was hard to dodge, secretly carried money to a central councilor to "handle it fairly," and emerged unscathed.}
{align:justify|Honest Tat Khoan, who knew nothing of bribery, was sentenced to four and a half years in prison. When the news reached Truong Si Luong on his sickbed, finding a lifetime's guarded honor destroyed in a single morning, he could not bear it and took his own life.}
{align:justify|Knowing Tuu Thanh to be cunning and scheming, Truong Kim Sung dared not break off the partnership.}
{align:justify|Tuu Thanh's cousin Chiem Moc Can was also in construction. Whenever Tuu Thanh landed a project he handed it to Chiem Moc Can to build, then leisurely demanded hefty commissions. But Moc Can's projects were never very profitable, and with such high fees to pay, his heart seethed with discontent. Kim Sung saw it and privately invited Moc Can to cooperate: Kim Sung would win the contracts and hand them to Moc Can to build, splitting the gains evenly, demanding no "rose or chrysanthemum fees" at all. Moc Can was delighted and gladly cooperated.}
{align:justify|When word reached Chiem Tuu Thanh, he was furious, feeling Kim Sung and Moc Can had repaid kindness with treachery. That very night he led six triad men to Kim Sung's house to accuse him and teach him a lesson. Little did he know the guards lay in ambush waiting, and the whole gang of villains was caught. In the end, besides being stripped of office, the notorious old fox was sentenced by the court for "instigating attempted murder" to a year and a half in prison.}
{align:justify|When Tuu Thanh had served his time and was released, he joined dark forces to open a construction company, bid for projects, hand them to builders, and skim the profits. Since he controlled countless projects, a great many failures and lawsuits arose. Each time trouble erupted, the police swore to investigate to the end — but after Tuu Thanh's secret dealings with them, the outcome always blurred into approval.}
{align:justify|Though Tuu Thanh slipped through the net of human law again and again, he could not escape Heaven's retribution.}
{align:justify|That day was his 65th birthday. His two sons, so their parents might enjoy a peaceful retirement, had specially pooled funds to build a magnificent villa as a birthday gift for their father — and his birthday was the joyous day of moving into the new house. But no one imagined that that very night the grand villa's foundations would suddenly subside and collapse, burying Mr. and Mrs. Thanh, the eldest son, and the factions that had partnered in the crooked contracts — none could escape, all buried alive beneath the rubble.}
{align:justify|Police investigation later revealed the birthday villa had collapsed from gravely stolen materials. Most astonishing of all, the crew that built it belonged to Mr. Thanh's eldest son's company.}
{align:justify|For Mr. Thanh and his contract-grabbing cliques specialized in pilfering materials, and he had passed this "masterstroke" to his son. His son in turn passed the "secret manual" to the partner builders. And once they had fully mastered it, they unfailingly used the same masterstroke to repay father and son. This is called the retribution of "plant melons, reap melons; plant beans, reap beans."}
{align:justify|In today's society, how many officials and construction companies sell their conscience for money, power, and fame? Look at Taiwan in the great earthquake of '92, and Sichuan and Wenchuan in the great earthquake — how many buildings, hollowed by stolen materials, collapsed and crushed countless unfortunate lives. These are the blood-soaked tragedies born of officials colluding with merchants in conscienceless construction.}
{align:justify|May all who read this true story keep their hearts turned toward goodness. If you use base tricks to win wealth, harming others is still harming yourself, and retribution is inescapable. For ill-gotten money — whether earned by your own hand or your descendants' — can never truly be enjoyed.}

{align:justify|Chiem Nguyet Quyen was a Taiwanese woman from Chiayi. In July 1979 she took her three-year-old daughter Tieu Man to Yokohama, Japan, and poisoned herself with mother and child together in a hotel.}
{align:justify|When police reached the scene, they found the word "HATRED" written in blood with a fingerprint. Both mother and child lay with eyes wide open, tears of blood still flowing — proof they had died in great pain and unwillingness. Japan's seasoned criminal police sensed at once that this suicide hid many twists — no simple matter.}
{align:justify|Japanese police took the case seriously. Tracing the dead woman's passport records, they spent a day with the Taiwan liaison office and quickly found the victim's parents. Quyen's father, Mr. Chiem Phong Binh, was a farmer; her mother Du Kieu Nguyet a housewife... Quyen was the third child — an elder brother and sister above, two younger brothers below. Bright from childhood, Quyen passed into famed Taipei Normal University, the admiration of the neighborhood and the pride of the Chiem family.}
{align:justify|In her fourth university year, joining a student corps activity, she met Uong Huyen Nhan of National Taiwan University — outwardly unremarkable, but richly talented, witty,eloquent, spilling pearls and jade with every word. Thus he won Quyen's heart.}
{align:justify|After half a year together, their affection deepening, they planned to marry upon graduation. In their friends' eyes they were a heaven-made pair — "the talented groom and the beautiful bride" — envied by all.}
{align:justify|But fate is unpredictable: the year Nhan graduated, he joined a mountain-climbing trip, and on the way back was suddenly killed in a car crash. While he lay in treatment, every day after teaching Quyen came to nurse him at hospital — where she met the intern Vu Luan Thuan. Thuan seemed a model of talent, quiet and taciturn. By then Thuan had already secretly fallen in love with Quyen.}
{align:justify|After Nhan passed away, Thuan pursued Quyen ardently. Whenever off duty at hospital, he came to her school, escorted her home, and cooked meals for her with his own hands.}
{align:justify|Each time Quyen returned to Chiayi to visit her parents, Thuan saw her off and met her at the railway station.}
{align:justify|Thuan courted Quyen patiently for a year and a half, until at last his devotion moved her and she consented to walk down the aisle.}
{align:justify|Only after the wedding did Quyen learn that Thuan had earlier applied to study in Japan and been accepted — the school required him to register within a month. Newly wed, Thuan wanted to give up the chance to stay beside his wife. But Quyen felt so rare an opportunity to study abroad must not be wasted, and as for tuition, she would find a way.}
{align:justify|Thuan agreed his wife was right and resolved to go, his heart full of emotion and gratitude.}
{align:justify|On the eve of parting, Thuan thrice promised his wife: once his doctorate was done he would return to Taiwan at once and open a clinic in Taipei, where husband and wife would work and live happily together.}
{align:justify|His words moved Quyen endlessly. She took pride in her husband's love and rejoiced that she had married the right man.}
{align:justify|From then on every burden fell on Quyen's head: she toiled to cover her husband's costly tuition and living expenses in Japan.}
{align:justify|A year later, every penny Quyen saved was sent for her husband's use. Besides teaching at school, she took private tutoring at home and at times asked her parents for money — yet still could not cover her husband's spending.}
{align:justify|By now Quyen's child was one year old, and the load grew heavier: the baby's keep, the husband's schooling — all pressed her until she could scarcely breathe.}
{align:justify|That winter, to solve their penniless straits, Quyen was forced to work in a wine-house, entertaining guests — "selling smiles, not her body" — for three years.}
{align:justify|Thus she covered her husband's expenses in Japan — but from years of drinking late into the night and toiling at teaching, Quyen contracted grave tuberculosis. Doctors declared she could never bear children again — a crushing blow. Yet Quyen kept faith: "All I have sacrificed is for my husband; he will surely understand and love me more"...}
{align:justify|As Thuan neared his doctorate, he returned to Taiwan for his grandmother's funeral. Learning Quyen had worked in a wine-house and caught lung disease, Thuan showed not a trace of anger nor pursued the matter — acting as if nothing had happened. Ever calm, ever tender toward his wife, he deepened Quyen's trust in his love.}
{align:justify|But Quyen never guessed that before returning to Taiwan, Thuan had secretly married his professor's daughter and opened a small clinic on the outskirts of Tokyo, with himself as director — the professor and his daughter having put up the investment, making them natural board members.}
{align:justify|Before long the news reached Quyen's ears. At first she disbelieved it as fabrication, yet half-believed it in her heart. So she resolved: once stronger, she would take little Tieu Man to Japan and learn the truth.}
{align:justify|Receiving his wife's letter, Thuan knew matters had turned grave. Lest his foul deeds be exposed to his shame, he secretly hatched a vicious plot.}
{align:justify|First, pleading busyness, he sent someone to meet mother and child at the airport, settled them in a hotel, and promised to visit that evening.}
{align:justify|Then he hired a man to pose as a waiter and, fifteen minutes before his own arrival, slip into the room and attempt to rape Quyen by force — at which point he himself would appear.}
{align:justify|The hired villain followed Thuan's plan: before Thuan he falsely claimed to be Quyen's lover, while Quyen, all too honest, stood mute as if dumb, unable to plead her innocence. Thuan sternly condemned her and loudly demanded a divorce.}
{align:justify|In the hotel, the play a complete success, Thuan at once turned his back on all conjugal feeling — wiping away four years of Quyen's grueling labor funding his studies, all that deep devotion, all her sacrifice, rendered meaningless in an instant.}
{align:justify|Seeing the marriage was beyond saving, Quyen choked back grief and rage and signed the divorce papers. Before the divorce in Tokyo, wanting little Tieu Man to see her father once more, Quyen traced school records to the clinic — and discovered the cruel truth: Thuan had long since remarried to a new love, and the hotel assault was a trap he had staged to destroy her. She felt the ground cave beneath her feet. In utmost fury she stormed to Director Thuan's house for plain answers — only to heap shame upon shame: Thuan poured every filth upon her and had the guards throw her out. Alone and helpless, Quyen never had a chance to confront him. Struck to the depths of body and soul, she took her child to Yokohama to visit an aunt, an overseas Chinese living in Japan, then returned to the hotel and poisoned herself together with little Tieu Man.}
{align:justify|Though Japanese police unraveled the case, mother and child were dead, and without witnesses or proof they could do nothing to Thuan.}
{align:justify|As for Thuan, hearing mother and child had killed themselves, he felt a load lifted, thinking he could now sleep soundly, free of trouble. So he went on exploiting his father-in-law's connections and prestige, chasing fame and profit in Japan.}
{align:justify|Later Thuan was invited to lecture at a school on "The Secret of Happiness Between Husband and Wife."}
{align:justify|Midway through his glib, triumphant lecture, the microphone for no reason suddenly smoked — then exploded. Unprepared, Thuan's head was split and bleeding on the lecture platform, both eyes blinded. Gravely head-wounded, he turned idiotic and senseless, and remains to this day under treatment and care in a Japanese hospital.}
{align:justify|Why should a microphone explode? Why should the victim be Thuan — the faithless, heartless husband who cruelly cast off the wife who was also his benefactress? And why should the lecture concern "conjugal love"? Was it coincidence, Heaven's punishment, or retribution?}
{align:justify|Tongues wagged on all sides.}
{align:justify|In this writer's view, Thuan escaped the law of nations and of men, but he could not escape the law of karmic retribution.}
{align:justify|Through this true story, may all married couples in the world be advised never to turn marriage into a pursuit of private gain. Such a marriage can never be happy. Doctor Thuan, for immediate fame and profit, stepped onto the wrong path and in the end lost even his conscience — so he paid the miserable price all have seen. This is called "evil causes meet evil retribution."}

{align:justify|One July morning in 1978, on a plateau in Taipei County, Thai A Vuong and his wife, together with their close friend Lam Thuan Xa, shouldered carrying poles up the mountain to pick lychees. Passing a long-abandoned cave, they suddenly saw three dogs dash out, their jaws dripping with human entrails still oozing blood. Curious, Mr. Xa crept inside to see what was happening — but the dreadful sight rooted him in horror: a gentleman over sixty lay in a pool of blood, chest and belly torn open, his innards carried off by those same three dogs. Madder still, sitting beside the corpse was a disheveled mad girl, playfully winding the victim's intestines around her neck.}
{align:justify|Mr. Xa's hair stood on end with fright; he spun around, fled the cave, and at once went with the Vuongs to report the crime.}
{align:justify|Police rushed to the scene. Apart from the hollowed-out chest and belly, the victim bore no other wounds. The puzzle: why was the mad girl beside him unharmed? Why had the dogs bitten the old man but not her?...}
{align:justify|Question upon question arose, and the mystery of the case seemed to defy the squad's professional skill.}
{align:justify|With the public's helpful cooperation, police swiftly learned the dead man was Lieu Ky Duc, and the mad girl Lieu Tu Tu — his daughter, now gravely mentally ill. They lived nearby in Shou Lin township.}
{align:justify|Records showed that in 1949 Mr. Duc had followed the Nationalist government to Taiwan, serving 28 years as a judge. Learned in law but crooked at heart, greedy for wealth and lustful to the extreme, he was a model corrupt official, retired a year before to live hidden in Shou Lin. A few of his deeds will suffice:}
{align:justify|In 1953, a Taipei customs official named Nguu Dong Doan, convicted of graft, was sentenced to ten years by the court of first instance. At retrial, the former judge having been transferred, Mr. Duc was appointed to hear the case.}
{align:justify|In court, seeing Mr. Doan's mistress Phan Chau in the gallery — a woman of striking beauty — Judge Duc stared without pause. He then deliberately blustered at Mr. Doan, threatening that ten years was far too light, and if new evidence emerged he would give at least fifteen!}
{align:justify|Terrified, Mr. Doan clasped his hands and begged the judge for leniency. But Mr. Duc refused.}
{align:justify|Days later Mr. Duc personally telephoned Mr. Doan's defense lawyer for a private meeting, hinting darkly that the sentence could be heavy or light — if the beauty Phan Chau would plead in person, perhaps it could be changed...}
{align:justify|The lawyer saw through Mr. Duc at once and promised to persuade Mr. Doan to let Phan Chau attend upon Mr. Duc for a week. Even then, Mr. Duc was unsatisfied and forced Mr. Doan's family to pay an enormous sum. Only when beauty and gold were both harvested to his liking did Mr. Duc declare in open court that evidence was insufficient, sentencing Mr. Doan to six months — convertible to a fine instead of jail. In effect, acquittal: Mr. Duc single-handedly freed the prisoner and covered the sky with one hand!}
{align:justify|Mr. Duc had come to Taiwan alone; after only a few years as judge, graft made him hugely rich. Through friends he married Thuy Dao, a farmer's daughter thirteen years his junior — gentle and lovely. The next year little Tu Tu was born.}
{align:justify|Sadly, when Tu Tu was two, Thuy Dao died of breast cancer. Busy with "official duties," Mr. Duc neglected the child. Once, when a cold brought a raging fever, she was not taken to hospital in time and became gravely simple-minded.}
{align:justify|His wife's death and his daughter's imbecility were two heavy blows. Yet Mr. Duc never once reflected that these were retribution for his lack of virtue. Instead he exploited his office all the harder to seize fame and profit and stuff his private coffers — even boasting loudly that with his power, rank, talent, and scheming, countless beauties would come offering themselves and gold and silver would pour into his pockets by themselves!...}
{align:justify|So through 28 years in power, day after day, month after month, year after year, he did nothing but outrage heaven and reason: extorting and blackmailing by every trick, manufacturing countless wrongful convictions and fatal cases.}
{align:justify|Many, wrongfully sentenced for his bribes, died of grief and sickness in prison while their wives killed themselves in protest. How many families were shattered because a man of high office had no conscience?}
{align:justify|During his judgeship, how many lives did he ruin, how many families destroy? His crimes piled mountain-high...}
{align:justify|In 1977 Mr. Duc finally retired, living hidden with a widow on the Shou Lin plateau.}
{align:justify|One morning, passing the cave with his daughter on the way to a festival, Tu Tu asked to rest inside a while. Indulging her, he stepped in — when suddenly three dogs charged at him, ferocious, baring sharp fangs and raking his chest and ripping his belly with savage claws. Within minutes they had dragged out all his vital organs, and Mr. Duc died in uttermost agony.}
{align:justify|The three "executioner" dogs carried out Mr. Duc's death sentence swiftly. Mission done, they vanished like lightning without a trace. Only Tu Tu sat beside the body, beaming with joy, calmly winding her father's intestines about her neck at play, looking as pleased as if she had just done something very fine.}
{align:justify|A judge's office is to uphold the law justly, enforcing the nation's statutes without fear or favor! But Mr. Duc, besotted with lust and loot, abused his power wholly for personal gain, betraying the trust of nation and people.}
{align:justify|His immediate retribution stands as a warning to the world: though you may dodge the punishment of human law, you can never dodge the retribution of cause and effect. As the saying goes: "Heaven's justice is utterly fair — you may hide for a while, but not for a lifetime."}

{align:justify|In 1981, after China's liberation, a hundred trades flourished, and remote Ta Hau village in Liaoning Province lived by growing and selling medicinal herbs. Some called the region the medicine village, famed throughout the nation.}
{align:justify|Eastern-medicine shops in the great cities all came here to buy herbs. There was a girl named Han Ha, whose parents both lived by growing medicine.}
{align:justify|Though only seventeen, Han Ha was outspoken. Seeing buyers come to the village and habitually cheat and squeeze prices, she stood up and rallied the villagers to unite for their common interest, representing the whole village in bargaining with the dealers and demanding fair prices.}
{align:justify|This helped the farmers earn more, so the villagers all praised and backed her.}
{align:justify|Do Nghieu, a great merchant from Shanghai, spotted Han Ha's talent and thought that with training she could surely earn him much money in future.}
{align:justify|Do Nghieu promised Han Ha a high post and high wages, inviting her to Shanghai. Burdened with aged parents and a brother crippled in both legs, Han Ha accepted Do Nghieu's offer.}
{align:justify|Taiwan sorely lacked Chinese medicine, so merchants flocked to the mainland to buy herbs cheap. One Tran Dong Tan had run a medicine shop in Taiwan since 1988, crossing to Shanghai every year for herbs, and thus knew Do Nghieu and Han Ha. Dong Tan had a younger brother, Huu Tan, who ran a marriage brokerage — introducing girls from the mainland and Southeast Asia to Taiwanese husbands. Huu Tan exploited his brother's connections, often leading unwed men to the mainland to seek brides.}
{align:justify|In November 1981, Huu Tan's company gained two clients: La Bang and Vuong Dong Nghia, both past thirty, looking every bit the capable models of men, both speaking boldly and engagingly. They wished to find mainland wives and promised high fees; Huu Tan pledged his utmost.}
{align:justify|Three months later Huu Tan led Bang and Nghia to Shanghai, lodging them at Do Nghieu's villa.}
{align:justify|That evening Do Nghieu feasted the three guests, with Han Ha and the waitress Vuong Hieu Hoan also present.}
{align:justify|At the feast, La Bang said he was general director of an electronics company with three branches and over two hundred staff.}
{align:justify|Busy day and night with work, he had been abandoned by his wife. Now he sought a clever, gentle, virtuous wife who could help him grow his career.}
{align:justify|Then Nghia introduced himself as owner of a construction company, busy traveling Taiwan's north, center, and south to manage projects; with no time for his wife, he had been divorced. Now he sought a kind, understanding woman for a wife.}
{align:justify|Bang and Nghia's glib self-praise, seconded by Huu Tan's flattery, drew Do Nghieu's ceaseless nodding approval.}
{align:justify|Han Ha, an unwed maiden, hearing them wove dreams... "These two great men of grand careers and double talents — just the perfect matches I have always dreamed of." Han Ha thought her family poor and herself uneducated; if either of them would not scorn her, she would consent at once — her life transformed, her family's poverty relieved, herself the general director's lady envied by all...}
{align:justify|The eyes are windows of the soul, and in Han Ha's gaze her hunger to change her fate shone plain; Bang and Nghia read it all... With Huu Tan chiming in and Do Nghieu's endorsement, the outcome was easily settled: Han Ha would marry Vuong Nghia, and La Bang would wed Hieu Hoan.}
{align:justify|While the two girls wove sweet dreams of married life, Bang and Nghia rejoiced inwardly — for they had barely cast the net and already landed the catch.}
{align:justify|Thanks to Huu Tan's eager help, the two brides were brought to Taiwan. The moment they stepped off the plane, Han Ha and Hieu Hoan were driven by the "Snakehead" gangster ring to a Taoyuan hotel, locked up, and stripped of their passports. Only then did Han Ha and Hieu Hoan wake from the dream, knowing they had fallen into a demons' den.}
{align:justify|The next day, Bang and Nghia came with the Snakehead boss Thai Con Lam to threaten Han Ha and Hieu Hoan. If the girls refused to receive clients or dared report to police, they said, not only would their own lives be in danger, their mainland families would be harmed too. To prove no empty threat, boss Lam produced photos of both girls' parents and described each family's home in perfect detail.}
{align:justify|Han Ha, knowing she could hardly escape the demons' clutches, endured and waited for a chance to flee. But Hieu Hoan could not accept the cruel reality; she screamed and wailed and raged as if dying and living again. The venomous Lam locked Hieu Hoan up and had his men rape and beat her. Body and soul brutally tormented, Hieu Hoan submitted to receiving clients — until a police anti-crime sweep returned her to the mainland. By then Hieu Hoan had caught a disease that left her sterile, and she is still under treatment.}
{align:justify|As for Han Ha, Lam not only forced her to receive clients without cease but lured her into drugs and gambling. Within one short year, the once young and lovely Han Ha had withered to yellow skin and bones — and finally collapsed; examination found tumors. Once Lam saw Han Ha had no more use value, he would waste no trouble on her and had the smuggling ring return Han Ha to her homeland. Whether she lives or dies is unknown to this day.}
{align:justify|As the Western proverb says: "Whoever does evil long, in his heart can know no peace." The three villains Lam, Bang, and Nghia specialized in luring and cheating mainland and Southeast Asian girls into prostitution in Taiwan. They profited hugely and lived in luxury — yet never knew peace. For night after night in sleep they were haunted by the girls' pitiful images: disheveled victims, hideous as demons, grinding their teeth in curses. At first only now and then, later every single night.}
{align:justify|In July 1995, for a whole month, all three thugs dreamed the same nightmare every night. They saw a leprous girl of ferocious face laughing most gruesomely. The laughter grew ever more terrifying; all three woke drenched in sweat. Next day all three felt the same sickness, so together they sought a famed spirit medium to exorcise the evil. The medium advised them to travel abroad — return home and all would be well.}
{align:justify|Finding this reasonable, four days later they flew to Thailand for pleasure, sure the nightmares would not follow. But on their very first night in Thailand, the moment they closed their eyes the demons appeared, plaguing them more savagely than in Taiwan — terrorizing their spirits further.}
{align:justify|A week later La Bang suddenly went mad. He was sent back to Taiwan and remains today in a psychiatric hospital in Changhua.}
{align:justify|Lam and Nghia fared even worse. While in Thailand they contacted no one — yet back in Taiwan both felt unwell, growing thinner and thinner, and Taiwan's hospitals could find no cause. They flew to America for examination and learned they had AIDS. Half a year later, racked by disease, their bodies ulcerated in utter agony, they died in America.}
{align:justify|Whoever practices goodness and stores virtue keeps a heart ever full of joy. Whoever does evil is scourged day and night by conscience, living in unrest — suffering ten thousand times worse than death.}
{align:justify|Let all who scheme to profit by forcing girls into prostitution look in this mirror: their end will be like that of Lam, Bang, and Nghia — in life a heart never at peace, in death utter torment.}

{align:justify|On Sungshan Avenue in bustling Taipei there was a roast-duck shop famed far and wide, called the "High-Class Delicacy." Business boomed, customers streamed in and out, the premises were vast and elegantly appointed, and the young male and female staff all wore white Western uniforms. The head chef wore a spotless white hat and gloves at all times, advertising utter hygiene.}
{align:justify|From dawn to night the staff spun like tops, receiving and serving diners. As for the owner, surnamed Thai, past fifty, round and plump in his Western suit, he sat beaming behind the cash register and money counter, a glittering gold watch on his wrist, grinning so wide his mouth would not close...}
{align:justify|Before the shop stood the retail counter; behind it lay the slaughterhouse and the duck-roasting ovens — guaranteeing duck meat always fresh, fragrant, and delicious, drawing diners back again and again. So trade flourished ever more.}
{align:justify|In recent years branches opened everywhere; the owner kept expanding his killing trade, and his circle of feasting companions grew — inevitably including patrons of wine-houses, dance halls, gold shops, casinos, and the like... This was the season of his utter self-indulgence, when he knew every climax of human pleasure.}
{align:justify|Each year when spring came and every household shopped for the New Year, with friends and kin exchanging gifts, the High-Class shop's business surged like a rising tide. Regulars wanting an oven-fresh roast duck had to queue three to five hours. Staff bustled till past midnight; the shop hired extra temporary hands yet stayed dizzyingly busy without rest — until the whole family was mobilized, all hands thrown into buying and selling.}
{align:justify|One day, like any other, the shop was buzzing and roaring with trade when suddenly, in a single instant, a tremendous duck-squawk rang out, drowning every other noise and seizing everyone's attention. Yet all who witnessed it stood frozen, rooted to the spot. In that moment they could only stare, wide-eyed, at old owner Thai sprawled on the floor, shaped exactly like a duck, his mouth ceaselessly crying "quack-quack" — not only like a duck's, but ten thousand times louder. The crowd pressed around, buzzing with talk, when a stout woman suddenly screamed:}
{align:justify|"Good heavens! This old man killed so many ducks he's getting his retribution! How dreadful! I'll never dare eat duck again!"}
{align:justify|Only then did everyone come to their senses and, as if of one mind, swarm out of the shop like a broken beehive.}
{align:justify|The lady manager hastily called doctors, but however skilled, no physician could stop that uncanny quacking. He cried so for three days and three nights, until, strength spent and voice gone, his eyes bulged wide, blood gushed from all seven orifices, and he breathed his last in uttermost torment.}
{align:justify|From that day the "High-Class Delicacy" sign was taken down, branches everywhere closed, and the Thai clan hid all trace of itself — none knows where they moved.}

{align:justify|In Liaozhong County, Liaoning Province, northeastern China, on an early-autumn morning in 1997, a gentle breeze blew; the Liao River lay calm, its water limpid green, grasses lush along its banks, branches swaying as the light wind passed. All was warm and gentle under the morning sun.}
{align:justify|Mr. Do, rod in hand and basket on his shoulder, strode briskly to the riverbank. He baited his hook and cast it in, then stared fixedly at the water, waiting in suspense, his face at once tense and delighted. Something like a big fish seemed to have taken the bait — the taut line and bending rod said so — and he jerked the line up. As the hooked creature broke the surface, Mr. Do's face shifted from joy to astonishment at the strange sight before him — one he could say he had never witnessed in all his years of fishing.}
{align:justify|The creature on the hook was no big fish but a great mother turtle. Clinging to her shell were several baby turtles, gripping their mother fast — so all were hauled up together. The moment the line touched ground, the little turtles acted like bodyguards: they ringed the great turtle at once, tiny black eyes glaring at Mr. Do, full of alarm. Bold and calm all his life, before this uncanny scene he did not know what to do.}
{align:justify|In truth he had heard tales of animals' spiritual nature, but such rumors and hearsay had never concerned him, nor had he believed a word — he always supposed people loved to exaggerate and invent.}
{align:justify|But now, facing this vivid yet unbelievable sight, he was utterly conquered. These tiny turtles reminded him of his own childhood, of the deep love of parents and siblings. And he thought these little turtles must be the same — knowing love and longing to protect their own. Surely they clung to their mother and loved her just as he had as a child. So the animals that humans slaughter without mercy also possess spirit, feeling, and lives just like our own.}
{align:justify|Mr. Do's conscience awoke, and at last he resolved to do something he had never done in his life: release captive animals! Quietly he unhooked the great turtle and set her in the water, then freed the baby turtles one by one after her, his heart full of remorse. He said to the turtles:}
{align:justify|"Forgive me — go home safely now!"}
{align:justify|The little turtles swam after their mother, and one baby kept turning back to look at him.}
{align:justify|Afterward Mr. Do snapped his fishing rod in two. From that day he never fished again and wholly gave up killing. He is now a faithful disciple of Buddhism, often retelling this story and urging everyone never to harm living beings.}
{align:justify|As the proverb says: "Man's nature at birth is fundamentally good." Our original nature is kind — but as the years grow and under the sway of unwholesome surroundings, that goodness is gradually veiled, and before we know it we have sunk into wrongdoing.}
{align:justify|The fortunate meet the conditions that wake them — reforming, abandoning evil for good, and recovering their own pure nature. Mr. Do is one of those fortunate ones.}

{align:justify|At the Hongri logging camp in Luhuo County, Sichuan, there was a worker surnamed Vuong who loved to fish and went down to the river whenever he was free.}
{align:justify|His way of fishing was unlike anyone's. Whenever a fish bit, he drew the line, unhooked the fish, and flung it straight up into the air; only when it crashed to the ground did he pick it up for his basket — so the living fish fell bruised and battered, dead if not gravely maimed. At the logging camp, once the trees were sawn, cranes were used to haul the timber to the road.}
{align:justify|One autumn day in 1979, Vuong was moving timber when he accidentally stumbled into the crane's working zone and was hooked up along with a log. He screamed, but no one heard. Hauled high into the air, he was spotted only when the crane operator noticed him and hurriedly lowered the crane. Too late: he fell from the height, smashed and broken, gravely wounded, blood pouring out, and lay dead on the ground — exactly like the hooked fish he had hurled skyward to crash down and die.}
{align:justify|Having witnessed it with their own eyes, the workers all called it karmic retribution, and most of those who loved fishing were frightened and dared fish no more.}

{align:justify|In 1997, a brilliant engineer designed and built a most unusual pig-slaughtering machine: feed a live pig into its mouth, and within minutes skin, bristles, flesh, bones, blood, head, and all would emerge, each portion perfectly cut and sorted.}
{align:justify|When the machine was assembled, on the day of its trial run all the company leaders and the designing engineer's family came to watch.}
{align:justify|The engineer started the machine himself as a demonstration. Confidently he flipped the switch. As he turned to step away, his clothing unexpectedly caught in the machine, as if someone were dragging him back; while he tugged with his hands, the machine's mouth seemed to exert a tremendous suction, drawing him straight inside. The onlookers, witnessing the horror, were stunned with terror.}
{align:justify|Someone rushed to cut the power — but too late. The engineer was no longer alive: for within minutes, true to his design, the slaughter machine swiftly fulfilled its mission, dividing his body into perfect portions exactly as its inventor had programmed — blood, flesh, bones, head, and innards all emerging neatly arranged in a flash. His colleagues could only stand and stare. His family wailed to heaven, all blindsided by what had just happened, unable to imagine that he himself would be the first test subject of his slaughter machine.}
{align:justify|Here we see humankind's selfishness, without a shred of mercy. Had it been a pig that fell into the machine just now, all would have cheered the successful experiment — and felt nothing like this grief.}
{align:justify|When the celebrated American writer Hemingway died, the whole world was shaken and mourned — for who could understand how so renowned a talent could take his own life, and with a gun at that? People found it utterly baffling.}
{align:justify|In fact, Hemingway in life loved hunting and shot countless animals with his gun — so in the end he used a gun to kill himself. In terms of karmic retribution, is this not the very answer?}

{align:justify|There was a laywoman who had received the Bodhisattva precepts, a Mrs. X, vegetarian for over thirty years. The whole latter half of her life was spent eating vegetarian and worshipping the Buddha. After she passed away, her family came to the temple to invite Master Nhan Ba Thiet to chant sutras and dedicate the merit to her. Naturally he gladly accepted.}
{align:justify|That night Master Nhan Ba Thiet suddenly beheld a most peculiar vision: a sow giving birth to a dozen-odd piglets, all white-furred — except for a single one with a black ring across its back. He saw it but paid it little mind.}
{align:justify|The next day Mrs. X's family came again to see Nhan Ba Thiet and asked:}
{align:justify|"Our mother ate vegetarian for many years, received the Bodhisattva precepts, and had a very good heart — so after her death, where will she be reborn? Might she have gone to the Land of Ultimate Bliss of Amitabha Buddha?"}
{align:justify|Master Nhan Ba Thiet replied:}
{align:justify|"Let me watch tonight's dream and see."}
{align:justify|That night Nhan Ba Thiet saw exactly what he had seen before: "that pig" in a brick-built pen in nearby Ngu On village — most clearly.}
{align:justify|On the third day Mrs. X's son came to inquire, and Nhan Ba Thiet, unwilling to speak plainly, only asked:}
{align:justify|"Before your mother passed, were there any signs?"}
{align:justify|Her son answered:}
{align:justify|"No, Venerable Sir. Near the end she lay unconscious, with no final words."}
{align:justify|Nhan Ba Thiet said:}
{align:justify|"Then it does not bode well."}
{align:justify|And the Master thought to himself: "Diligent Buddhist disciples usually have some foreknowledge, some lucid understanding. A lifelong vegetarian like Mrs. X, good-natured, fond of helping others, and devout in offerings to the temple — how could she at death be reborn as an animal? How could that be?"}
{align:justify|Master Nhan Ba Thiet said nothing to Mrs. X's son, only instructing:}
{align:justify|"Go home and investigate — ask the whole family whether your mother said anything before she died. For before passing she still had lucid moments; she must have spoken of something, mentioned something — such as a wish she longed to fulfill but never could, and so clung to it in her heart. Go home and ask."}
{align:justify|The son obeyed and investigated at home. He learned that on the evening three days before her death, his wife had nursed the old lady and heard her say over and over:}
{align:justify|"Slaughter that pig — quickly!"}
{align:justify|She repeated it again and again. Then she died.}
{align:justify|Hearing the family's account, Master Nhan Ba Thiet said:}
{align:justify|"Now it is clear. I can confirm: her fall into this realm today comes entirely from clinging to a deviant thought."}
{align:justify|Then Master Nhan Ba Thiet told Mrs. X's kin:}
{align:justify|"Quite far from your home lies a village called Ngu On, where a family raises pigs. There a sow has just farrowed a litter of a dozen-odd piglets. Go there and you will see one piglet most special and unlike the rest. Buy it quickly and bring it back — I will give it refuge."}
{align:justify|He did not yet dare tell them outright that it was their mother's new body.}
{align:justify|They searched for three days until they reached Ngu On and found the piglet with exactly the coloring Master Nhan Ba Thiet had described. Stranger still, the moment the piglet saw them it ran over, delighted and friendly. Seeing the pig's manner, Mrs. X's kin needed no words — each understood in silence and guessed this must be their mother. Unspoken, they grieved. They quickly bought the pig, brought it to the temple, and asked Nhan Ba Thiet to give it refuge.}
{align:justify|Refuge given, her children discussed taking it home to raise. Nhan Ba Thiet said:}
{align:justify|"No. Refuge taken, it must be carried deep into the mountains and released."}
{align:justify|The family worried that once released, fierce beasts would devour it.}
{align:justify|Nhan Ba Thiet said:}
{align:justify|"Would you rather have her stay in the animal realm forever?"}
{align:justify|The family took it to release, and before long it died. The pig dead, she was reborn into the human realm.}
{align:justify|Nhan Ba Thiet said: "Let this be a lesson to warn you all: though a lifetime of reciting the Buddha's name and eating vegetarian, if one keeps uttering deviant views and clinging to an unrighteous mind without release, it is supremely dangerous at the hour of death — and one must repay one's own evil thought. A deviant thought can strike its owner with rebounding retribution: however one plots to harm people or creatures, one is harmed first oneself. Why did your mother, before dying, keep demanding the pig be slaughtered? Because in her heart she nursed one thing constantly: hoping that when her eldest grandson married, she would follow Taiwanese custom — slaughtering pigs and goats and holding a lavish wedding feast. This was the heart's wish she forever cherished, carried, and longed to fulfill for her grandson's wedding."}
{align:justify|Since the wish went unfulfilled — the grandson unwed while the grandmother died — the deviant intent lingered in her heart. So at the dying breath, whichever thought in the mind is strongest leads one to rebirth in the realm matching that mind. Near death her heart was all blazing killing-thoughts; she had no longing to recite Amitabha Buddha's name and beg His reception — her mind was all bloodlust, craving to slaughter the pig. Nursed to ripeness, this deviant thought drowned a lifetime of merit and wholesome thoughts, and she had to be reborn as a pig and repay her own bloodthirsty intent.}
{align:justify|Then Master Nhan Ba Thiet concluded:}
{align:justify|"A lifetime of reciting the Buddha and eating vegetarian — fallen, for not releasing deviant views. Deathbed karma is most terrifying. Be careful, be warned!"}

{align:justify|People in Shanghai have a saying ever on their lips: "retribution in this very life" — meaning what you do in the present is repaid in this present life, without waiting for the next. In Shanghai there was a Mr. Vuong who loved eating turtle meat, but his way of cooking was most peculiar: he filled a pot with water and sealed it with a lid — a hole bored in the lid's center, just big enough for a turtle's head. He put the live turtle in, then lit the fire. As the water slowly heated, the turtle, tormented beyond bearing by the boiling, poked its head through the hole, gasping for air — whereupon he poured his pre-made, still-boiling seasoned sauce into the turtle's mouth, forcing it to pull its head back in. His aim was to let the seasoning soak through the turtle's whole body, making the meat delicious. But never did he spare a thought for the turtle's suffering: inside, boiling water scalding its vitals; outside, roasting flames. Upon the living turtle he inflicted torment from within and without — utterly savage. Nor was that all: writhing and thrashing in agony in the hot water, unable to bear the boiling torture, with no door to flee by and no road to life, it could only poke its head through the lid's hole to cool off. Then cruel Mr. Vuong poured on more heated sauce — salt, oil, vinegar, chili, pepper — and the turtle, its insides burning, jerked its head down. The poking out and pulling in repeated again and again until at last the turtle died in uttermost torment. For greedy love of tasty food, Mr. Vuong could kill so ruthlessly.}
{align:justify|Then Mr. Vuong enjoyed his turtle dish. A dozen days later, one night as he slept in his upstairs room, the ground floor suddenly caught fire. Flames devoured the staircase and spread to his bedroom, reaching the bedclothes before he startled awake. In confusion and panic he meant to flee downstairs — but the wooden stairs had burned away. Below lay a sea of fire; no way down. He tried to squeeze through the window to save himself, but the iron bars' gaps were too narrow: strain as he might, again and again he could not pass — only his head poked through, wedged fast there, unable to advance or retreat.}
{align:justify|As he screamed, the fire engines came. Fierce flames flooded the ground; his head, trapped in the iron bars as if roasting hotter still, gaped wide, howling for rescue, while the fireman aimed an unceasing jet at the barred window — and scalding water shot into his mouth, painful exactly as when he had tormented the turtle long before. The head stuck beyond the bars kept gulping boiling water, and then perhaps he truly tasted the pitiful torment the turtle had known. And so Mr. Vuong died at last in the sea of flames.}
{align:justify|His way of killing and eating the turtle made him taste an identical torment — karmic retribution never lies. As we use an evil heart to kill creatures in life, that very evil heart harms us in turn. Mr. Vuong cast himself into the furnace of water and fire.}
{align:justify|The Dhammapada records:}
{align:justify|"When evil meets with fortune, its wickedness is not yet ripe; when the evil fruit ripens, one suffers cruel punishment oneself." Mr. Vuong's reaping of retribution truly chills the heart; the manner of his death was pain beyond bearing. Know that however we kill any animal, an equal retribution returns. May all who love killing reflect and take heed.}

{align:justify|There is a monk 65 years old who tells of his youth, before ordination, when he loved to hunt. He tells his story thus:}
{align:justify|"In 1978, I lived in Songpan County, Sichuan, earning my living by growing medicinal herbs and hunting. One day the three of us went hunting together. Rifles on our backs, hounds in hand, we came to a certain forest — and saw in the trees a troop of golden-haired monkeys. We hastily raised our guns. The golden monkeys were terrified, but with hunting dogs below, escape was impossible. Monkeys hit by bullets died in the trees; those hit but not dead fell and were mauled by the hounds.}
{align:justify|Just then I saw a mother monkey carrying her baby. As I aimed my rifle, ready to fire, the mother hastily set her baby aside, pointed at it, then waved her hands at me — then pointed at herself, signaling as if asking me to shoot her. I understood: the mother would sacrifice herself, willing to die, begging me not to kill her child. The mother monkey moved me; tears welled up, and I hastily lowered my gun.}
{align:justify|That evening my companions cooked monkey meat, but haunted by what I had seen, I would not eat. In my heart I reproached myself without cease. Why crave monkey meat? Why heartlessly kill them and sell their skins and bones? I had slaughtered lives so cruelly — while the monkeys' feelings differed nothing from humans'. Alas! What guilt!}
{align:justify|The monk also told of his friend, a crack shot who loved smashing birds' nests. The birds he caught he sold or drowned in wine — countless beyond number. He also loved catching eels, fish, frogs, turtles, and softshells — even poisoning fish with chemicals, killing them most cruelly.}
{align:justify|Later a huge ulcer rose on his body; doctors called it a malignant tumor. Worse, all over him broke blisters big as pigeon eggs, and before long his flesh rotted as he lay in bed screaming with pain.}
{align:justify|One rainy night he went outside, and carelessly fell dead into a ditch, none knowing. Later his wife searched and found his floating corpse with countless turtles, softshells, frogs, fish, and birds fighting over his flesh.}
{align:justify|The monk says:}
{align:justify|"From then on I abandoned hunting entirely and vowed never to kill."}
{align:justify|His face sinks into thought, into remorse. He goes on:}
{align:justify|"I only wish all hunters could lay down their guns, still their killing hearts, and understand that animals too cling to life and fear death, and feel love and sacrifice just like humans. Once killing's retribution arrives, it is most dreadful."}

{align:justify|Mr. and Mrs. Hua were merchants living in Malaysia, with a son named Vu Hung. Hung would not study diligently but loved bad company, roaming and idling outside day after day.}
{align:justify|Mr. Hua could only send his son to England to study, hoping that far from his bad friends he might yet turn out well.}
{align:justify|In England, Hung remained unchanged, roaming everywhere day after day, spending money on pleasure.}
{align:justify|Until Mr. Hua's business failed and money ran short, forcing him to call his son home from his studies. But Hung was already addicted to drugs: far from helping his father's enterprise, he sold off the house and all of the old man's remaining property. Mr. Hua sickened with grief, and before his dying breath he repented and confessed a truth no one had ever known:}
{align:justify|Long ago, he had been secretary to a rich merchant — but he coveted his employer's wife. Once, while his master was home visiting his native village, he laid a trap, staging a car accident that killed his master horribly. And so all his master's possessions, fortune and wife alike, fell into his hands.}
{align:justify|Every wish fulfilled, Mr. Hua and the merchant's widow fled far away to build their lovebirds' nest elsewhere.}
{align:justify|A year later they had a son — the precious Vu Hung himself.}
{align:justify|And in the end, after squandering his father's money, Hung sold off the whole family estate, until even after his father died he could not afford a coffin. His mother too died of tumors. The Hua line fell utterly into "house ruined, lives lost."}

{align:justify|A Chinese immigrant woman, call her Lan, went to Brazil to build a life and volunteered at a hospital. After three months of training, Lan was assigned to the morgue — work most people feared and dared not take.}
{align:justify|Lan was a devout Buddhist disciple. Whatever the state of the dead — unclaimed, tragically killed, wrongfully killed, suddenly dead — all received her thorough care. Chanting the Buddha's name, she bathed the departed, changed their clothes, and the like... Lan volunteered at this good work for long years on end.}
{align:justify|Ten years passed; her husband's workplace closed, and the couple moved to the city to find new work. They started a small trade. Before long they met a close friend who kindly invited them to act as agents for a Taiwanese computer-parts shop. They accepted.}
{align:justify|They worked diligently, business gradually prospering, and within a few years they had become millionaires.}
{align:justify|This Buddhist disciple never let success, soaring like a dragon in clouds, make her forget her duty. Lan kept giving money and effort in offerings to the Three Jewels and monasteries, and contributing to hospitals, patients, and social charities.}
{align:justify|Because husband and wife diligently created blessings and stored virtue, their children too turned their hearts to goodness like their parents — filial and talented both. After graduating from university, they not only built successful careers, contributing no little to nation and society, but carried on their parents' charitable vows, ever storing virtue and creating blessings.}
{align:justify|Reading this far, we can understand: as the cause, so the fruit — good deeds meet good retribution. All is sown by us and reaped by us.}
{align:justify|May all understand the law of cause and effect clearly, diligently creating wholesome conditions, wholesome causes, wholesome enterprises. May all hearts be bright, wisdom unfold, and, advancing with vigor, guard body, speech, and mind, steering themselves along the wholesome path, so life is ever happy.}

{align:justify|In the autumn of the Mouse year, under the Kangxi Emperor of the Qing dynasty, the family of Truong Nguyen in Beijing bought a donkey. It was most vicious by nature — fond not only of kicking but of biting people too. Only the three members of Truong Nguyen's own family could ride it; whenever they mounted, it behaved most meekly and tame.}
{align:justify|But once, a Mr. Duong came to borrow the donkey and try riding it. Strangely, the donkey docilely let Mr. Duong ride away.}
{align:justify|That night Mr. Duong dreamed he saw a man in black saying:}
{align:justify|"I am the donkey in Truong Nguyen's house. In a past life I borrowed three hundred coins from you and never repaid them, so in this life I must repay you. Yesterday you rode me 280 miles; tomorrow morning, please ride me 20 miles more, and the debt I owe you will be settled."}
{align:justify|Mr. Duong asked: "Then how much do you owe the Truong family?"}
{align:justify|The man in black looked sorrowful and said:}
{align:justify|"Much, very much — beyond counting!"}
{align:justify|Waking, Mr. Duong went to the Truong house to borrow the donkey and rode a long stretch — when suddenly the donkey bucked and threw him off. Mr. Duong reckoned it up: he had ridden exactly 20 miles. Finding the affair truly marvelous, and the donkey most remarkable, Mr. Duong told it:}
{align:justify|"Now I understand and believe why you let me ride. But this place is ten miles from my home — if you won't carry me, how shall I get back? Let us agree: I shall ride you home, then spend ten strings of cash on hay for you. Agreed?"}
{align:justify|The donkey stood there gazing at Mr. Duong a long while, then consented to carry him home.}
{align:justify|(From the Buddhist Grace Gazette, volume 4)}

{align:justify|Original Chinese text: Tran Thu Ngoc}
{align:justify|This is a true story of real people and real events that took place under my country's Song dynasty; its heroine was later reborn in Taiwan. I invite you to read patiently and learn more of karmic retribution.}
{align:justify|Under the Song there was a princess named Thang Hoa, most proud by nature, just turned nineteen, unwed, of peerless beauty.}
{align:justify|One day, in Chieu Hoa palace, she heard her father and mother speak of the newly crowned top scholar Manh Dat — a young man gifted in poetry and prose, and moreover a fine lute player.}
{align:justify|Afterward the emperor invited the new top scholar into the palace to play for princes and nobles, summoning empress, concubines, princesses, and princes of the three palaces and seventy-two courts to Duong Sinh hall for wine and music. At the king's command Manh Dat played and sang, his voice warm and melodious.}
{align:justify|All were moved and applauded together. Concubines and princesses buzzed with remarks about Manh Dat. Princess Thang Hoa alone, seeing his double gifts of talent and bearing — lofty, handsome, and gallant — admired him deeply.}
{align:justify|One day the emperor and empress were discussing Thang Hoa's marriage just as she arrived. The emperor said: "You come at the perfect hour — we are discussing your marriage. Have you a beloved in mind?"}
{align:justify|The princess shyly replied: "I am only eighteen, still so young — why must Father hurry...?"}
{align:justify|The empress beside her hastened to say: "Eighteen is hardly a child — I entered the palace to serve His Majesty at sixteen, more than twenty years ago now. 'Grown sons take wives, grown daughters take husbands' — delay only breeds sorrow, and one day you will regret your lateness!"}
{align:justify|Pressed by the empress, the princess could only say:}
{align:justify|"Today I saw the new top scholar Manh Dat — young, brilliant, most promising, clearly a man of gifts, peerless in letters, and playing and singing so well... truly admirable!"}
{align:justify|Hearing Thang Hoa praise Manh Dat without cease, the emperor and empress knew at once the princess favored him, and secretly sent the prime minister as matchmaker to wed the pair.}
{align:justify|Two days later, just as Manh Dat came to court to visit the prime minister, the latter asked:}
{align:justify|"The young, brilliant top scholar, of uncommon character and looks — may I ask whether you are married?"}
{align:justify|Manh Dat replied: "This humble scholar married over four years ago!"}
{align:justify|The prime minister shook his head:}
{align:justify|"What a pity! What a pity! Today His Majesty, seeing you handsome and gallant, of gifts beyond men, wishes to wed Princess Thang Hoa to you — what do you say?"}
{align:justify|Hearing this, Manh Dat stammered anxiously:}
{align:justify|"You mean... His Majesty would choose me as prince consort?"}
{align:justify|"Exactly, Mr. Scholar! Consider well: wed the princess, become prince consort, and a lifetime of glory and riches without end is yours!"}
{align:justify|Manh Dat thought at once of his poor household, where even three daily meals were a great worry — even the money for his journey to the capital examinations had come from his wife cutting off her hair to sell. To return to that bitter poverty of old, he wanted nothing... And Princess Thang Hoa's beauty was utterly enchanting; what man would not crave such a dragon-climbing, phoenix-perching chance? So he agreed.}
{align:justify|Before long, Manh Dat's wedding to the princess was celebrated in splendor. On the wedding night, as Manh Dat lifted the bride's veil and saw the princess so beautiful, smiling coyly at him, he was beside himself with triumph. Fresh-crowned top scholar and instantly chosen prince consort — truly: "Ten years poor and friendless, one day famed and courted!"}
{align:justify|Since that one examination trip, three years had passed without his return. He had clean forgotten the old wife at home, waiting and yearning — who had sacrificed her lovely hair, shearing it to sell for his journey. A truly faithless man, forgetting the old for the new.}
{align:justify|One day My Loan, Manh Dat's former wife, heard rumor that Manh Dat now held office. Overjoyed, she hastily borrowed two taels of silver from neighbors to travel to the capital and reunite with her husband.}
{align:justify|On the road My Loan tasted every hardship, till at last she reached the capital. But there, heaven-crushing news awaited: Manh Dat was now prince consort, living happily with Princess Thang Hoa. Bitterer still was thinking of herself, forlorn in the capital with not one familiar soul... Betrayed by her husband for a new love, she ached to weep yet no tears would come.}
{align:justify|Then she thought: "Now that I am in the capital, come luck or ruin, I must risk it and try to see my husband."}
{align:justify|Asking around, she learned the prince consort's mansion lay west beyond the walls, and went straight there. Manh Dat being away, it took her three visits to find him. The moment Manh Dat saw her, his face betrayed utmost alarm, and he hastily lodged her with a family nearby.}
{align:justify|From then on Manh Dat often hid it from Thang Hoa and secretly visited his former wife. Three months later My Loan was with child — and before long the rumor reached the princess's ears.}
{align:justify|Thang Hoa blazed with fury but knew not what to do, so she discussed it with her trusted maid A Kieu. A Kieu said the former wife must at once be "granted death" to avoid "long nights and many dreams." The princess found this reasonable and ordered A Kieu to find My Loan.}
{align:justify|A Kieu hastened to Manh Dat's trusted page to ask after his former wife's whereabouts. At first the page would not tell, but A Kieu gave him ten taels of silver, and the dazzled page confessed: "Manh Dat's former wife lodges with Truong Tam's family east beyond the walls, not far from here."}
{align:justify|Delighted with the news, A Kieu hurried back to report to the princess, who ordered A Kieu to invite My Loan to come.}
{align:justify|Poor My Loan had once lived with her husband in their country village, tasting hard, lean years — yet husband and wife were deeply devoted. Since Manh Dat was learned and gifted, playing and singing well, the villagers admired them greatly.}
{align:justify|Later, to fulfill his ideals, Manh Dat journeyed to the capital for the examinations. At one sitting he took first place as top scholar. But unhappily he caught the princess's favor and was chosen prince consort. One examination trip, three years without word — so My Loan trekked a thousand miles to find her husband.}
{align:justify|After Manh Dat settled her with his close friend of the Truong family, he visited My Loan daily. Before long My Loan was with child. Manh Dat's heart rejoiced; thinking he would soon be a father, he merrily told his close friends. But secrets would not keep: the rumor spread to Thang Hoa's ears, and she raged, ordering her trusted maid to find My Loan and settle accounts.}
{align:justify|A Kieu reached My Loan's lodgings just when the Truongs were out, with only My Loan at home. Seeing My Loan graceful and lovely, fair-skinned with long hair, smooth of limb, looking so leisurely and pampered... A Kieu thought privately: "Just the look of a petted favorite! No wonder the prince consort dotes on her and cannot forget his beloved wife!"... A Kieu seethed, but feigned ignorance, saying sweetly:}
{align:justify|"May I ask — is My Loan at home?"}
{align:justify|My Loan was startled to see before her a girl in rich dress, surely no commoner's daughter — yet why seek her? Puzzled, she still answered:}
{align:justify|"I am My Loan — who are you? What brings you to me?"}
{align:justify|A Kieu said: "I am Princess Thang Hoa's maid. The princess sends me to invite you to enjoy glory and riches — come with me at once to the prince consort's palace, and lodge no longer in this mean place."}
{align:justify|My Loan, overjoyed to hear it, hurried with A Kieu by carriage straight to the princess. The princess, seeing My Loan come, rejoiced inwardly, thinking:}
{align:justify|"Ah! This hateful My Loan — now watch how I punish her..."}
{align:justify|For her part, reaching the prince consort's mansion, My Loan was enraptured. The mansion was vast and magnificently grand. She saw Princess Thang Hoa seated high above, maids lined in long rows on both sides, the princess's servants bustling in and out. Though the princess was peerlessly beautiful, jealousy made her look fierce and commanding, and My Loan trembled inwardly. A sense of foreboding rose in her heart.}
{align:justify|Thang Hoa asked My Loan:}
{align:justify|"Are you My Loan, the one Manh Dat married in the country?"}
{align:justify|My Loan hastily replied:}
{align:justify|"This humble woman is indeed My Loan, Manh Dat's wedded wife. Because he stayed in the capital so long without returning, I came to find him that we might reunite. I beg the princess's grace to fulfill this humble woman's wish."}
{align:justify|At these words the princess erupted, slamming the table with her order:}
{align:justify|"Here are three hundred taels of silver — take them and crawl back to the country. From this day cut all ties with Manh Dat; you are forbidden ever to meet him again!"}
{align:justify|Hearing this, My Loan grieved bitterly and burst into sobs:}
{align:justify|"I want no silver — I want only my husband!"}
{align:justify|Seeing money was useless, the princess reflected: "Only My Loan's death can end this trouble, as A Kieu advised" — and shrieked:}
{align:justify|"You hateful wretch! Refusing the toast, you would drink the forfeit. Kindly I grant you money, and you scorn it — you will be the death of me! Very well! Guards! Drag this bold wench out and kill her for me!"}
{align:justify|Seeing the princess act so, My Loan in fury blurted:}
{align:justify|"Thang Hoa, you are vile! You stole my husband and now would kill me to silence me. Though I become a ghost, I shall find you and have revenge!"}
{align:justify|But the princess's household did not wait for My Loan to finish — they dragged her off, slew her, and buried her body in the meadow beyond the walls.}
{align:justify|Not long after, strolling outside, the princess chanced to overhear two maids whispering of how she had killed her rival. Greatly vexed, she knew not how to hush it. Her wet nurse beside her offered counsel:}
{align:justify|"My lady, why not kill every servant who knows of that day's affair? Lest the prince consort learn of it one day and your married happiness crack..."}
{align:justify|The princess found the nurse's words reasonable and summoned a dozen-odd maids, secretly sentencing them all to death. (Among them the maid A Nga was the elder sister of A Dan; the two sisters served together in the princess's palace. Bound by deep sisterly love,) }
{align:justify|when A Dan heard the dreadful news that the princess meant to kill her sister, she rushed before the princess to beg A Nga's pardon.}
{align:justify|A Dan knelt before the princess, weeping: "I beg you, my lady! Open your merciful heart and spare my sister — I will serve as your ox and horse all my life — I bow and beg the princess not to kill my sister..."}
{align:justify|Thang Hoa's temper was already foul, and she paid A Dan's plea no heed, waving her away — but beside the princess stood a small table, and her sweeping hand struck the vase upon it. It fell... and smashed hard into A Dan's head. A Dan shrieked piteously, blood streaming over her face... The princess stared in shock and hastily called physicians to carry A Dan off for treatment. But the bleeding was too great, and days later A Dan died.}
{align:justify|Poor prince consort Manh Dat from first to last knew nothing of the beautiful Princess Thang Hoa's cruel deeds. She had cunningly covered and buried every trace.}
{align:justify|A dozen-odd years later Princess Thang Hoa died, and Manh Dat followed her in death.}
{align:justify|Reborn together in Taiwan}
{align:justify|In the 38th year of the Republic (1949), prince consort Manh Dat was reborn into the Ngo family of Tainan, named Dan Hung. At five or six, Dan Hung learned the violin — for his lute talent from the past life still lay latent. The teacher needed teach but once for Dan Hung to grasp tenfold. Moreover Dan Hung's playing soon surpassed his teacher's, its sound incomparably beautiful.}
{align:justify|By coincidence, Thang Hoa was likewise reborn into a farming family of Kaohsiung, named My Le. Time flew; in the blink of an eye twenty-odd years passed, and My Le had grown into a clever, lovely young woman. Dan Hung in this present life studied music a dozen-odd years, then went abroad — to Europe, to learn violin under famed masters. Accomplished, he returned home, renowned as a master player.}
{align:justify|Before long, friends introduced him to My Le. Two years of friendship followed. They found each other perfectly matched, affection growing ever closer. So they married and moved to Taipei. My Le and Dan Hung's nest was like any ordinary couple's.}
{align:justify|Years later My Le met many good friends who guided her to take refuge in the Three Jewels, and she became a devout Buddhist disciple. All day she guarded her speech and ate vegetarian. Moreover she often visited temples to create blessings with offerings.}
{align:justify|Understanding the Buddha's teaching deeply, My Le always urged her husband to take refuge in the Three Jewels — but Dan Hung would not listen, daily drowned in the five desires. My Le grieved deeply, often reciting the Buddha's name before the shrine, praying the Bodhisattva would manifest and wake her husband.}
{align:justify|One day Dan Hung's friends gave him a huge crab. Dan Hung was delighted, thinking tonight would bring a fine meal.}
{align:justify|Seeing it, My Le sighed inwardly. Knowing her husband's temper well — that huge crab would surely end in his belly that night — she silently prayed to Avalokiteshvara Bodhisattva to save the crab.}
{align:justify|Daylight passed swiftly and night fell in turn. My Le avoided touching the crab and calmly retired to prepare for sleep, but Dan Hung told her:}
{align:justify|"You sleep first — I must go to the kitchen to cook the crab for nourishment."}
{align:justify|Hearing it, My Le grieved inwardly, pitying the crab about to be eaten by her husband; silently she prayed for Avalokiteshvara Bodhisattva to manifest and save the poor creature, so her husband would not eat it.}
{align:justify|Dan Hung went down to the kitchen, set water on, and lit the stove. As he prepared to seize the crab for the boiling water, at that urgent moment a miracle suddenly appeared.}
{align:justify|Dan Hung stared at the crab in amazement, not knowing why it kept circling inside the bucket; when Dan Hung reached to grab it, it hastily raised its trembling claws... looking utterly terrified.}
{align:justify|Watching, Dan Hung stood transfixed, and a thought flashed in his heart: "How selfish I am — for a moment's tasty pleasure to end this poor crab's life. How pitiful this crab is!" And so he resolved: at daybreak tomorrow he would release it.}
{align:justify|Meanwhile My Le in the bedroom fretted ten thousandfold, not knowing how the crab fared, so she kept reciting Avalokiteshvara Bodhisattva's name to pray for the crab. After a dozen minutes, still uneasy, she resolved to go down to the kitchen to find Dan Hung.}
{align:justify|Scarcely out the door, she met her husband at the bedroom entrance. She asked Dan Hung: "Did you eat that crab?"}
{align:justify|Dan Hung shook his head: "No! I did not eat it! At dawn tomorrow I shall release it. Go down to the kitchen and see — the crab still lives."}
{align:justify|Hearing it, My Le half-believed, thinking it impossible — her husband had been so eager for the tasty dish; how could the crab have escaped?... Just now he had insisted on killing the crab — why now release it? My Le wondered greatly, and thinking, walked into the kitchen to look — and truly the crab still lay in the bucket.}
{align:justify|My Le went to the bedroom to question her husband. Dan Hung told her all that had just happened, and My Le suddenly recalled how the scene matched two lines of the Universal Gate chapter of the Lotus Sutra: "Reciting Avalokiteshvara's power, at once a kindly heart arises."}
{align:justify|This was surely the awesome might of Avalokiteshvara Bodhisattva manifest to save the crab. Next morning husband and wife climbed into the car together, Dan Hung driving to release the crab.}
{align:justify|It was spring; trees were green, branches bursting with tender buds like new lives beginning. The car stopped beneath a great tree; My Le lifted the crab's bucket from the car, and the two prepared to free it into the stream. They recited Avalokiteshvara Bodhisattva's name, chanted the Great Compassion Mantra, and recited Amitabha Buddha's name... Hearing the holy names of Buddhas and Bodhisattvas, the crab looked most delighted. Only then did My Le and Dan Hung see how endearing this crab was, and Dan Hung realized how cruel he had been to eat so many pitiful water creatures. Now, through freeing this crab, he understood: animals too have feelings, yet humans daily roast, grill, steam, and stew these lovable beings... Truly unbearable.}
{align:justify|My Le set the bucket down; the crab crawled out, raising both claws, its manner as joyful as a farewell bow — as if thanking them for the grace of saving its life. Seeing the crab's gratitude, Dan Hung was deeply moved and silently resolved: from this day he would eat vegetarian for life alongside his wife.}
{align:justify|My Le had a younger sister who often felt pain in her lungs. Later she went to hospital for X-rays; doctors said there was no illness — yet the films showed two great holes, none knew how. Then My Le's sister recalled how as a child she used to tear the wings off mole crickets and dragonflies for fun, even poking two holes through their bodies — and now the two holes on the X-ray looked exactly like the holes she used to bore in dragonflies.}
{align:justify|My Le had a second elder brother who at thirty suddenly lost the use of both legs. Doctors could find no cause. Later My Le gave him a sutra of Ksitigarbha Bodhisattva; reading it deeply, he suddenly remembered how as a child he used to break the legs of crickets, dragonflies, mantises, and other small creatures. Hence the retribution of legs that would not move. This is just what the Ksitigarbha Sutra says: "If one maims living beings, one suffers the retribution of calamity and crippling."}
{align:justify|Seeing her brother's and sister's karmic retribution, My Le often thought of her own heaped-up killing — not counting the many lifetimes of birth-and-death transmigration past, this life alone was frightening enough. For before knowing the Buddha's teaching, in ignorance she had killed a great many; now she secretly feared, none knew when retribution would fall upon her own head.}
{align:justify|Because her husband had once loved rich food, savoring every meat and seafood, she had often killed chickens and ducks, slaughtering every kind to please him. The ancients said: "The gentleman stays far from the kitchen" — surely for this reason! For the kitchen sees so much slaughter that the gentleman cannot bear to watch or hear, and so does not enter it.}
{align:justify|Once My Le understood the Buddha's teaching, she learned of repaying and retribution for causes. Through her killing karma, soon after taking refuge in the Buddha she fell ill, seeking cures from Eastern and Western physicians across the province — yet the illness would not heal.}
{align:justify|Dan Hung's friends, hearing My Le was ill — and the government having opened the door allowing Taiwanese to visit the mainland — suggested the couple cross to the mainland for treatment.}
{align:justify|So husband and wife flew to Thailand, and thence to Beijing. Aboard the plane they planned first to tour the sights — Tiananmen, the emperor's palace... — and only then enter a Beijing hospital.}
{align:justify|The plane had just landed in Beijing when the couple found a city guide to lead them first to Tiananmen. And right there, a strange affair unfolded.}
{align:justify|Tiananmen Square is vast; the couple walked a good four hours till their legs ached. Then My Le left Dan Hung to find the public toilets.}
{align:justify|Entering the lavatory, she suddenly saw a flickering red glimmer dart past — most uncanny and frightening. She supposed her eyes were dazzled... Done, she told Dan Hung, who scolded her roundly: "You insisted on trudging and trudging till you're worn out — now your head swims and your eyes play tricks, mistaking chickens for hawks, east for west!"}
{align:justify|But when Dan Hung entered the public lavatory and pushed the door open, he too suddenly saw a red glimmer flash — and a trace of fear rose in his heart, as if meeting a matched and mighty foe. Done with the toilets, My Le's head suddenly ached as if to burst. Dan Hung and the guide could only escort her back to the hotel.}
{align:justify|Halfway there, My Le's face grew ever paler. Along the road she wept without cease. Dan Hung could only bring My Le to hospital. In the Beijing hospital, doctors gave My Le medicine and she fell into deep sleep.}
{align:justify|Next day, when Dan Hung reached the hospital, a nurse drew him aside and whispered:}
{align:justify|"Mr. Ngo, your My Le is behaving most strangely — go in and see for yourself!"}
{align:justify|Dan Hung entered his wife's room to find My Le stretched on the bed, her voice most strangely changed, as if another's. She said:}
{align:justify|"I am a maidservant of the Song court, once attending Princess Thang Hoa! Lam My Le's former self is Princess Thang Hoa herself, and you — Ngo Dan Hung — are the prince consort, her husband of that past life. Because Princess Thang Hoa murdered My Loan, the prince consort's former wife, she also slew a dozen-odd maids who knew of it, to silence them. We ghost-maidens have sought Princess Thang Hoa so very long; today at last you have delivered her body to us. Now we demand her life — she must repay us..."}
{align:justify|The ghost-soul finished, and My Le writhed in torment, wailing without end.}
{align:justify|Hearing the wronged ghosts, Dan Hung trembled uncontrollably with chills. He thought the nurse must surely have heard these uncanny words already. Frightened nearly to flight, he still forced himself to be bold, mustering courage to speak firmly:}
{align:justify|"I... I beg you wronged ghosts to spare my wife this once. Whatever you demand, we shall grant it all — only spare my wife's life."}
{align:justify|The ghosts said:}
{align:justify|"Forgiveness comes not easily — she killed us so miserably! Many suffered wrongful, unavenged deaths! You ask us to spare her — would that not be far too easy for her? Do you know, once she had killed us, we fell to the hells, enduring a hundred thousand torments in prison? Not long freed from hell, our band fell into the ghost realm, drifting masterless, riding the winds that curl around Tiananmen... Now we have met Thang Hoa reborn as My Le — we seek her to repay the hatred of past lives!"}
{align:justify|Hearing the ghosts' furious account, Dan Hung said:}
{align:justify|"I see no better way: you have suffered greatly — let me have My Le pay for rites and chant a full Emperor Liang Repentance to deliver you. Only grant My Le her life, and by the merit of the Emperor Liang Repentance you shall soon be delivered..."}
{align:justify|The ghosts said: "Agreed — had you not pleaded so earnestly today, we would surely demand her life. But now you have promised...? Then keep your word! If not, we shall return to seize her in revenge! Now we go..."}
{align:justify|My Le's voice returned to normal as before, but she sank into deep coma. Dan Hung hastily called the doctors; treated, her face slowly regained color. My Le lay in hospital a dozen-odd days, her body gradually mending. All the while Dan Hung stayed faithfully beside her, nursing her well. Half a month later doctors discharged My Le.}
{align:justify|Seeing his wife well, Dan Hung said: "They say Beijing has many antiquities — my friends told us that coming here we must at least buy some antique to bring home."}
{align:justify|Three days after My Le's discharge, Dan Hung took her to an antiques stall.}
{align:justify|It was a famed antiques stall. But another uncanny affair arose: the moment the couple arrived, the staff warmly invited them to browse. Dan Hung admired the lovely antiques displayed in rows, lifting a porcelain bowl to examine — when the owner hastened to say:}
{align:justify|"Sir, you have a connoisseur's eye — this is a Ming emperor's Nine-Dragon Jade bowl, most precious, for eating rice. If you fancy it, I shall cut the price and sell it cheap! Only fifteen thousand Taiwan dollars — about 440 US dollars!"}
{align:justify|My Le beside him flared at this:}
{align:justify|"One tiny bowl at such a price — outrageous! Are you drunk, shopkeeper? Fifteen thousand Taiwan dollars, about 440 US dollars! While your mainland workers earn only six or seven US dollars a month — do you think we Taiwanese are all rich, to name such a sky-high price?"}
{align:justify|Hearing his wife's scolding, Dan Hung felt she was right. He went on browsing other wares. On and on the two walked... Suddenly My Le saw a pair of most beautiful flower vases about 25 centimeters tall — and abruptly her face showed utter terror, her voice changing most strangely:}
{align:justify|"I don't want to see the vases! Princess Thang Hoa struck my head with this vase — so much blood kept flowing... flowing and flowing... till death... In this life I fear nothing more than seeing flower vases! I want no more of it — I am so afraid, so afraid... Quickly! Quickly! Take the vases away, take them away now! I don't want to see them — I don't want to see these dreadful vases!..."}
{align:justify|Only now did Dan Hung know the ghost-soul upon My Le was speaking. He knew not what to do. Seeing My Le faint, he hastily telephoned for an ambulance to bring his wife to hospital.}
{align:justify|The doctors said: "Your lady was discharged not long ago; still undernourished, and now shocked — she must be readmitted, or her life is in danger. Best take her back to Taiwan, to a great hospital for emergency care."}
{align:justify|Helpless, Dan Hung obeyed the doctors and at once flew home.}
{align:justify|Chanting sutras to pray for the wronged ghosts}
{align:justify|Back in Taiwan, Dan Hung rushed My Le into National Taiwan University Hospital, but the doctors could find no cause for My Le's coma and could only sustain her with IV fluids. Seeing his wife sink daily, Dan Hung hastened to find Master An at temple X and invite the temple's monks and nuns to assist in reciting for My Le's rebirth.}
{align:justify|They chanted two days and two nights. But My Le from first to last stayed comatose and unwaking. Seeing her so, Master An asked Dan Hung:}
{align:justify|"Did anything uncanny befall your wife on the mainland? If not, why does assisting recitation change nothing? Try hard to remember!"}
{align:justify|Dan Hung thought long and hard, then told everything that had happened on the mainland, each thing in turn. Master An asked again:}
{align:justify|"And after you returned, did you chant the Emperor Liang Repentance to deliver the wronged ghosts?"}
{align:justify|Dan Hung shook his head:}
{align:justify|"No! While my wife lay in hospital I was busy nursing her, with no time to visit temples and invite the Sangha to chant the Emperor Liang Repentance to release the ghosts..."}
{align:justify|Hearing this, Master An said:}
{align:justify|"Your wife's illness is surely karmic creditors of the past come to claim their debt — the ghost-souls you two met on the mainland. They must have suffered in the three evil paths. Now that you two have learned to cultivate, the wronged ghosts have specially come to demand you dedicate merit and chant the Emperor Liang Repentance to deliver them — hence your wife cannot be reborn! Best make a vow to deliver the wronged ghosts. Otherwise even doctors are helpless and cannot cure your wife..."}
{align:justify|Hearing it, Dan Hung's eyes brimmed with tears. Thinking a while, he prayed:}
{align:justify|"Our wronged ghosts! Do not demand my wife's life — only let her heal and grow strong, and I shall surely invite masters, monks, and nuns to chant a full Emperor Liang Repentance to deliver you."}
{align:justify|The promise made, suddenly My Le stirred and began to breathe. Seeing My Le improve, Master An and the monastics bade Dan Hung farewell and returned to the temple.}
{align:justify|My Le lay in hospital three whole months, Dan Hung growing gaunt with nursing her. After discharge, Dan Hung helped My Le home to convalesce.}
{align:justify|Before long she gradually recovered and grew strong.}
{align:justify|To help them keep their promise to the wronged ghosts, Master An held a great Emperor Liang Repentance ceremony at the temple. Besides Dan Hung and his wife, My Le's sister and brother also attended. The whole family chanted the Emperor Liang Repentance together.}
{align:justify|All chanted resoundingly:}
{align:justify|"Upon the Emperor Liang Repentance assembly, flowers rain down... The repentance rite chanted through, sins are wiped away! May we meet again together in the Dharma assembly..."}
{align:justify|The great repentance ceremony had run just over an hour when something strange happened: cold sweat broke over My Le's head, big as beans, drop chasing drop; she grew dizzy and faint. Seeing his wife's pallor, Dan Hung hastened to ask:}
{align:justify|"What is it — what is wrong?"}
{align:justify|My Le could stand no longer, nor answer; her face turned black. Dan Hung told My Le's sister:}
{align:justify|"My Le looks so ill — hurry into the temple and ask the Sangha for a place where My Le may rest. Perhaps, so soon out of hospital, chanting the repentance tires her... Hurry and invite Master An — something may happen. I shall help My Le to rest first..."}
{align:justify|My Le's sister hurried to invite Master An, who showed them a resting place for My Le. Scarcely lying down, My Le began tossing in agony on the bed. Dan Hung and her sister knew not what to do and hastily asked Master An what was best.}
{align:justify|The Master answered:}
{align:justify|"According to the sutras, chanting the Emperor Liang Repentance can help ghosts ascend to the heavens — but if one of deep karmic obstacles, or one whom wronged ghosts claim, joins the Emperor Liang Repentance assembly, one falls ill, or the head aches and the body pains..."}
{align:justify|Dan Hung asked Master An again:}
{align:justify|"Venerable Sir, when will my wife's condition heal?"}
{align:justify|Master An shook his head:}
{align:justify|"Hard to say — each person's karmic obstacles differ. So the illness's course differs too; when your wife's illness turns, none can know."}
{align:justify|At that moment My Le groaned aloud in pain, a sound of horror shaking heaven and earth; all could only stand and watch, helpless, knowing not what to do.}
{align:justify|Suddenly My Le's voice changed, her accent most strange, wailing piteously:}
{align:justify|"I am My Loan, wedded wife of the prince consort, husband of Song-dynasty Princess Thang Hoa. Thang Hoa not only seized my husband but slew me miserably. I died most wrongfully, my heart full of hatred, so I cried my wrong before the court of Yama, King of Hell. The King pitied my plight — husband lost and murdered too — and granted me leave to find Thang Hoa, now reborn as My Le, and claim my debt in revenge! Now, Lam My Le! Repay me with your life!"}
{align:justify|All present were seized with terror, bones rattling. This is just what the sutras mean: "When causes and conditions gather complete, the fruit must be repaid."}
{align:justify|Master An stepped forward to counsel:}
{align:justify|"My Loan, I beg you to be generous — raise high and strike light, and spare Lam My Le her life. For My Le now knows to eat vegetarian and cultivate. She vows to chant a full Emperor Liang Repentance for you, hoping you may ride upon this merit to ascend to the heavens — no need to seek her in revenge and add more entanglement. Spare her — will you?"}
{align:justify|The wronged ghost said:}
{align:justify|"In her past life as Princess Thang Hoa, this woman murdered countless innocents — and through life after life wallowed in eating animals, slaughtering chickens, ducks, fish, pigs, and more... Her crimes tower like Mount Sumeru, just as the Ksitigarbha Sutra says: 'Those sins so great, deep as the vast sea, obstructing the holy path'... How then could the merit of chanting one tiny Emperor Liang Repentance possibly counter and wipe away such heavy sins?"}
{align:justify|Master An, feeling the wronged ghost spoke truly, gently counseled:}
{align:justify|"True — you are not wrong. Though My Le's grievous sins are great, consider: the scriptures also teach that 'only let one hold a repentant heart, sincerely repenting the evil karma created through distant past lives, resolving to reform toward goodness, striving in cultivation, creating merit, diligently chanting the scriptures, devoutly holding mantras or reciting the names of Buddhas and Bodhisattvas — and, relying on the awesome power of Buddhas and Bodhisattvas, heavy sins may be repaid lightly'..."}
{align:justify|The wronged ghost wept:}
{align:justify|"If the Master reasons so, then my deep hatred of so many years has no way to be avenged? I am not content — I must have revenge."}
{align:justify|Master An explained:}
{align:justify|"I do not mean to wrong you! Consider the present: it is your very deep hatred that has kept you in the three evil paths. Such suffering leaves you no way to be reborn. Now your enemies — My Le and her husband — deeply repent; they have learned to store virtue and practice goodness, to eat lifelong vegetarian, sincerely cultivating and mending their faults... So I beg you to be generous, open your heart in forgiveness, lighten your mind, and let it ascend and soar... that you may enjoy the happiness and peace you deserve... Seek their debt no more. I urge you to turn hatred into kind forgiveness — it will bring you the greatest benefit. For a good mind summons a good realm. From now on, often visit temples for Dharma assemblies, diligently hear the sutras and cultivate, and grant your enemies a chance to cultivate and redeem their faults, creating merit to dedicate to you, that you may be reborn into a wholesome realm. Would that not be far better? All is mind-made; release hatred and you need suffer in the three evil paths no more. Do you agree?"}
{align:justify|Hearing Master A's explanation, the wronged ghost softened:}
{align:justify|"Had the Master not spoken today with such sense and feeling, I would never spare her. Now, Lam My Le, remember well: this debt I shall reckon later — if you do not cultivate properly, if your repentance is not sincere — you must make a great, great deal of merit to dedicate to all us wronged ghosts. If not, we shall return to torment you cruelly. Now I go!"}
{align:justify|The wronged ghost gone, My Le recovered as before. Resting a while, she chanted the Emperor Liang Repentance on with the assembly. The Dharma assembly ended, and My Le gradually regained health and strength.}
{align:justify|Half a year passed. One evening My Le lay in her room when suddenly all the creatures she had ever killed and eaten — chickens, ducks, fish, pigs, and more — came thronging to claim her life, mauling her... My Le screamed in terror. Seeing it, Dan Hung rushed to recite the Buddha's name and chant the Rebirth Mantra. Just then My Le suddenly saw the great crab husband and wife had once released come hurrying to restrain the beasts and plead for her. My Le's head ached as if to burst. She hastily recited the Buddha's name, and Dan Hung hastily brought the recitation device. The two kept reciting, reciting on and on.}
{align:justify|Neither knew how long they recited, until they saw a radiant beam of light and the image of Amitabha Buddha, immense, manifest to receive all those creatures. When they saw them no more, husband and wife ceased reciting.}
{align:justify|The tale of My Le and Dan Hung — once princess and prince consort in a past life — became widely known and retold among northern Taiwan's Buddhists. This account is written from those tellings. May all who read it gain benefit.}

{align:justify|"This is a story a thousand times, ten thousand times true. I present it to you not so you may marvel at its strangeness, but to prove that in this world there truly are the six realms of rebirth and the phenomenon of karmic retribution — and moreover that this happened in our own time, in Taiwan. This record was written in Mai Lieu village.}
{align:justify|Why this record was written}
{align:justify|Around February this year (the 50th year of the Republic), Master Tinh Van accepted an invitation to lecture on the sutras at Ho Vi, accompanied by Master Chu Van. With the days free, several of us lay followers went with the two masters to tour the neighboring villages near Ho Vi.}
{align:justify|Meanwhile, while Master Tinh Van lectured, Nun Tri Dao was busy at Mai Lieu, and since I had never been to Mai Lieu, I wished to visit its sights.}
{align:justify|Mai Lieu is a coastal land, its transport inconvenient, with nothing much to see. After visiting Nun Tri Dao at Tu Van Temple, we meant to return to Ho Vi — but the abbot of Tu Van Temple insisted we stay for lunch. With the bus already missed, we stayed on at the Thuong Lieu Thien hall — and it was there, prompted by Mr. Hua Ti Thach, that this strange tale came up.}
{align:justify|This story is one of borrowing a body to return to life. It happened long ago, but its owner steadfastly refused to retell it, so very few knew of it — only residents near Mai Lieu village. People elsewhere, meeting or hearing mention of it, stayed in the dark, for no one ever told them the wondrous tale; some even deemed it impossible. For it is a little-known story — even the person herself did not wish to raise it.}
{align:justify|When we first heard it, the telling was disjointed and unordered, sounding tangled and confused. Though we heard little and understood less, the words "borrowing a body to return in spirit" gripped us, and we longed to get to the bottom of it. So we hurried through lunch and rushed straight back to Ho Vi, determined to interview the story's principals at any cost.}
{align:justify|Chu Tu Hoa borrows a body to return to life}
{align:justify|The heroine of this uncanny tale now lives at house No. 95, Trung Son Street, Mai Lieu village. The house is a building-materials shop; its owner is Mr. Ngo Thu Dac, and his wife Mrs. Ngo Lam Cuong. When we arrived, the wife was out in the fields and Mr. Ngo was busy working; learning our purpose, his face showed clear displeasure. After the three of us greeted him in turn, he reluctantly told us what had happened. He said:}
{align:justify|"It happened in the 48th year of the Republic. I earn my living in construction and had joined the building works at Dao Hai Phong on Dai Tay islet. While there I seldom came home. Chancing home once, I found my wife ill — but when I went back to Dao Hai Phong, her illness eased. Later I came home more often, and my wife's illness grew ever graver, until the Dao Hai Phong works were finished and I returned for good — by which time my wife's sickness was past curing. Her illness was not so dangerous in itself, but her mind was then abnormal; she kept raving and raging. We meant to take her to a psychiatric hospital, but she refused. Even joining forces we could not seize her, and she kept shouting:}
{align:justify|'Do not take me to the asylum — I am not mad! I am from Kinmen, my name is Chu Tu Hoa!'}
{align:justify|Moreover her accent had utterly changed — but I never believed my wife's body had been occupied by another's soul.}
{align:justify|Mr. Ngo fell pensive as if recalling the past, his gaze resting on the couple's photograph, sighing softly before going on:}
{align:justify|"I truly cannot fathom how so strange a thing could happen in this world! Still less could I imagine it falling upon my own house."}
{align:justify|Pausing a moment, he continued:}
{align:justify|"While I worked on the construction, every day cycling home from Dao Hai Phong, I always felt something heavy on my shoulders — but supposing it was the steep road, I paid it no mind."}
{align:justify|Only later did I learn: each time I came home, that Kinmen maiden — riding behind my bicycle — clung to me all the way home.}
{align:justify|At this the gentleman would say no more; pleading tea-making, he ended the tale.}
{align:justify|Just then, his nephew — his elder sister's son, a young man over twenty — was helping us and chatting along, when Mr. Hua, our guide, arrived, saying he would go find Mrs. Ngo for us. He told us many wished to meet her, but she refused them all. So this time, whether she would grant a meeting, he dared not guarantee. Still, he would do his utmost to find her.}
{align:justify|Mr. Ngo's nephew told us:}
{align:justify|"While my aunt was ill, I always helped my uncle nurse her. Sometimes my aunt wept, sometimes she muttered things we could not understand. Often, lying down, she would suddenly sit bolt upright; my uncle and I together pressed her back to bed — but then her strength was immense, and far from pinning her down, we were flung off instead."}
{align:justify|I thought: how could a woman be so strong? In truth it was my aunt's "friends" helping her. At this the nephew looked mysterious; I knew by "friends" he meant other spirits.}
{align:justify|He went on:}
{align:justify|"When we understood that Aunt Cuong's body had been entered by another soul, we knew not what to do. We could only let her rest and mend. At first she found everything unfamiliar. When my uncle called her A Cuong, she protested:}
{align:justify|'My name is Chu Tu Hoa, not A Cuong!'}
{align:justify|When her mother and sister came to visit, she insisted:}
{align:justify|'I do not know you people — who are you?'}
{align:justify|Of course she recognized none of our neighbors. (Here the nephew glanced warily inside, as if fearing his uncle might suddenly appear and overhear.) He lowered his voice:}
{align:justify|'Toward his family my uncle is most responsible — but he and his former wife (the old A Cuong) clashed constantly and lived unharmoniously. Yet my uncle was always upright, never seeking girlfriends or affairs outside.'}
{align:justify|But that time, at the Dao Hai Phong works, many laborers said they saw a girl keeping close beside my uncle, and they would tease:}
{align:justify|'Who would have thought Mr. Ngo was such a player!...'}
{align:justify|Once the eldest workman, resting, raised the matter of the girl beside my uncle, hinting and joking that he was a lucky man — but my uncle looked utterly baffled. He flatly denied it, saying he never brought any woman to the site. Deny as he might, the workers never stopped gossiping... My uncle supposed they were idle, teasing him for fun. So he ignored what everyone said.}
{align:justify|Little did he dream that the 'girl' everyone saw beside him then is our present aunt (Chu Tu Hoa). Truly, before entering Aunt A Cuong's body, Chu Tu Hoa followed beside my uncle every day.}
{align:justify|He lit a cigarette and went on:}
{align:justify|'Strange indeed to tell: the Dao Hai Phong works employed a great many hands, but before, every contractor who took it on lost money, and laborers at the site kept falling. But once my uncle contracted the works, not only did he earn a great deal, every worker stayed safe. Who knows — was it the spirits of Dao Hai Phong secretly sheltering and blessing us?'}
{align:justify|By now Mr. Ngo had brought out several cups of tea, and over tea we listened as his nephew continued:}
{align:justify|'You may not believe it, but this is what I lived through myself — telling it still frightens me. It went like this: when my aunt (Chu Tu Hoa) had just recovered, she often said friends came visiting and bade us prepare chairs and incense to receive them. But whenever we obediently did as she ordered, no one ever came — yet we heard her talking as if guests had arrived, chatting away merrily, laughing as she spoke. Stranger still, the bamboo chairs looked for all the world as if people were truly sitting in them, for they creaked and groaned.'}
{align:justify|Until my aunt said farewell to the guests, when the chairs sounded again, as if people were rising to leave. It was as though the unseen, fearing my aunt would be lonely, had come to visit and keep her company. But after that one time, they never came again.}
{align:justify|Since recovering, my aunt (Chu Tu Hoa) seems to know everything — she is literate, she can do anything. Compared with the old Aunt Cuong, who could not read, her bearing and conduct differ as heaven from earth — like two utterly different people.}
{align:justify|In the old days my former aunt could only cook rice and nothing else. But since falling ill and mending, she has changed completely.}
{align:justify|My present aunt not only works the fields but shoulders all the other heavy labor too. As for cooking, she says she never did it. It is all backwards and most strange. Nor is that all: every taste, gesture, shape, and step is utterly changed. Of course her voice and accent changed entirely — my aunt's speech today is pure Kinmen dialect.}
{align:justify|At this Mr. Ngo sipped his tea, looked around, and listened to our talk. His nephew pointed at the statues of Avalokiteshvara Bodhisattva and Ksitigarbha Bodhisattva enshrined on the altar and told us:}
{align:justify|'My uncle used to worship only his ancestors — these Buddha and Bodhisattva statues were invited home for worship by my aunt (Chu Tu Hoa). Let me tell you: my'}
{align:justify|former aunt loved eating fish and meat, but since the soul changed bodies, my new aunt not only dislikes fish and meat — put anything savory before her and she absolutely will not eat. So she always eats separately, never with the family.}
{align:justify|Just then Mr. Hua returned outside, and our eyes searched for the story's 'heroine' to see if she would come with him. He shook his head: "Alas, 'she' would not come — and wept so bitterly!"}
{align:justify|So Bao Phung and I followed layman Hua out to persuade Chu Tu Hoa to return.}
{align:justify|For our coming had quietly wounded Chu Tu Hoa's heart. When we saw her, she seemed drained of strength, leaning against a neighbor's gatepost. Eyes tightly closed, tears streaming down. I thought she must have sat here weeping a long while. Only after long comforting could we help her home.}
{align:justify|This time our visit reminded her of her Kinmen family, so her heart brimmed with unceasing sorrow. Though she strained to talk with us, after one or two words she burst into tears and could utter no sound.}
{align:justify|That day she strained to tell us in broken tones: "Her name was Chu Tu Hoa, her home in Kinmen; her father Chu Thanh Hai, her mother Thai Diep. She was 18 then. War having broken out in Kinmen, she followed the crowd aboard a fishing boat to flee. Then the boat drifted long at sea; all ran out of food and died of hunger and thirst. At last she too fainted. After none knew how long, she woke to find the boat drifted to the sea islet of Dai Tay. She was rescued alive — but afterward the fishermen pushed the boat back out to drift away"...}
{align:justify|At this she covered her face and fled inside. We longed to know more, but seeing her so stricken, we could not press her further. Time too was short — we had to catch the bus back to Ho Vi — so we all rose to bid the household farewell.}
{align:justify|Leaving, we promised that next time we came to Mai Lieu we would give Chu Tu Hoa a Buddhist rosary.}
{align:justify|Seizing gold and murdering, repaid at once}
{align:justify|Mr. Hua came with us. On the road to the bus stop he told us more:}
{align:justify|"Chu Tu Hoa in flight might well have survived. The moment the fishermen rescued her, she said: 'I beg you, save my life — whatever it takes, as bride or as servant I can serve... and all the money aboard I give to you'... But that fisherman was too wicked and utterly conscienceless! He seized the gold and flung the person into the sea... Yet in the end he could not live in peace to enjoy the ill-gotten gold. For before long his whole family died one by one! Now only one son remains — a little mad boy, dreadfully insane. Ah! Buddhism's 'karmic retribution' is mistaken not at all!"}
{align:justify|At this he looked over us all and went on:}
{align:justify|"Strange too to tell: when Chu Tu Hoa recovered, someone carried the news to Dai Tay, and Dai Tay folk hearing it were all amazed, calling it too strange. Some knew well how, years before, that mad boy's family robbed and murdered this girl — so they led the mad little boy (the robber's son) to meet Tu Hoa. To everyone's surprise, the moment they reached the gate, Chu Tu Hoa barred them from entering, weeping: 'Has your family not harmed me enough that you come to grieve me more?'"}
{align:justify|Old A Cuong had never been to Dai Tay, and when the mad boy came, no one there knew the affair in detail — yet Chu Tu Hoa recognized it all at a glance. That is truly strange.}
{align:justify|Gifting the rosary, revisiting Mai Lieu}
{align:justify|Around July this year, layman Hung Cu Minh came to Ho Vi to teach fellow devotees to chant Buddhist hymns. In conversation, Master Chu Van mentioned this 'borrowed body returning in spirit' affair. Layman Nang heard it with great interest... and since I had promised Chu Tu Hoa a rosary, I seized the chance to bring Layman Nang to Mai Lieu.}
{align:justify|Layman Nang had once lived in Kinmen, so Kinmen matters were familiar to him. On the road he told me many Kinmen tales — architecture, trades, customs, folkways, and more...}
{align:justify|These were all materials to clarify things when meeting Chu Tu Hoa.}
{align:justify|That day the weather was foul; drizzle met the bus on the road. I feared the rain would worsen — but when the bus reached Mai Lieu the rain had stopped. I could not help rejoicing, silently reciting in my heart: "Nam mô A Di Đà Phật!"}
{align:justify|All Kinmen matters remembered}
{align:justify|With rain, Chu Tu Hoa had not gone to the fields, and learning she was home, my heart lifted as of a burden dropped.}
{align:justify|Perhaps because I brought several people, Chu Tu Hoa hesitated long before coming out. But this time she seemed far calmer; seeing us, she smiled and nodded greeting — though the smile was strained.}
{align:justify|First I gave her the rosary, then chatted idly. With last time's lesson, I did not broach the matter directly but circled round it, beginning with religion.}
{align:justify|Chu Tu Hoa said:}
{align:justify|"As a child I deeply believed in the Buddha — I even ate lifelong vegetarian. Busy as work is now, morning and evening I worship the Buddha. I know well: the Buddha's words are never wrong. Each person should do good and never do evil — for evil reaps no good fruit!"}
{align:justify|Last visit I had heard neighbors say Chu Tu Hoa worshipped the Buddha diligently every day, and I thought: "Surely this is why she could return in spirit to the human world!" So I seized the chance to ask:}
{align:justify|"You say you believed deeply as a child — is there a Buddha Recitation Hall in Kinmen?"}
{align:justify|She thought a moment, then answered:}
{align:justify|"I do not know. But in my house we worshipped the Buddha and Avalokiteshvara Bodhisattva; I worshipped only at home — my whole family worshipped the Buddha."}
{align:justify|I asked:}
{align:justify|"Do you still remember Kinmen things now?"}
{align:justify|She sighed:}
{align:justify|"Alas! I remember! All of it! But it is past — why raise it?"}
{align:justify|"If someone now wished to help you find your parents, would you consent?"}
{align:justify|"Of course, I would rejoice. But who would help me? And even found, I fear my kin would not know me!" — she smiled bitterly and went on: "For the body I wear now is not the form I wore leaving Kinmen!"}
{align:justify|At this she pressed her lips tight and her eyes reddened, but she strained not to weep before guests.}
{align:justify|I pointed to Layman Nang seated below and said:}
{align:justify|"This gentleman lived long in Kinmen; moreover he believes in the Buddha and knows Kinmen well. Tell him of drifting at sea, and relate it truthfully. Afterward..."}
{align:justify|At this two round tears like pearls appeared in her eyes, and she hastily wiped them away.}
{align:justify|I interrupted, continuing for her:}
{align:justify|"Afterward they seized your money and flung you into the sea — so their whole family later died out, leaving only a mad little boy..."}
{align:justify|Before I could finish, she cut in:}
{align:justify|"Ah! You have heard this too?! Truly, the gold aboard was not all mine, but belonged to many fellow refugees. And that they seized the gold and then their whole family died out is true. But I believe in the Buddha: though I know them ruthless and conscienceless, I nurse no feud with them. This reckoning comes from my fellow voyagers' indignant dead, avenging the wrong!"}
{align:justify|I asked her again:}
{align:justify|"Before coming to Mr. Ngo's house, where did you dwell?"}
{align:justify|The question displeased her somewhat, but at last she answered:}
{align:justify|"I dwelt at Dao Hai Phong village on Dai Tay, where green trees abound and the sea is most beautiful. I lived there many years."}
{align:justify|"Did you like the place?"}
{align:justify|"I liked it; I stayed there long. Cast into the sea, my soul lingered round Hai Phong islet. I stayed there some ten days before Lord Vuong took me as his follower. Lord Vuong told me my lifespan was not yet ended — I might borrow the body of Ngo Thu Dac's wife to return to life. He bade me lodge meanwhile at the Bac Prince Temple. Before long Ngo Thu Dac came to Dao Hai Phong for work, and I followed Ngo Thu Dac..."}
{align:justify|When Ngo Thu Dac finished his work and returned home, I followed home too, to await my chance. Days later Mrs. Lam Cuong Yeu's illness worsened, her soul departing to another realm. I seized the chance to borrow the body and return in spirit.}
{align:justify|Pausing a moment, she went on:}
{align:justify|"But borrowing a body is no easy thing. Entering another's frame is most anguishing work. Thanks to Lord Vuong's aid, it took over twenty days to complete."}
{align:justify|"How did it feel then?"}
{align:justify|"Most natural, yet unfamiliar" — meaning before she had been a maiden, and now must wear the body of a married elder woman.}
{align:justify|I changed the subject:}
{align:justify|"Is Mai Lieu as fine as Kinmen?"}
{align:justify|"Mai Lieu? How could this place compare with Kinmen? Kinmen houses are all built of brick, its roads flat and straight. Where we lived was a merchants' quarter — bustling and lively. But Mai Lieu houses are built with no order, all jumbled and chaotic!"}
{align:justify|Layman Nang agreed with her.}
{align:justify|By Layman Nang's reckoning, Chu Tu Hoa fled in the 43rd year of the Republic, for in that year many saw the army shipping guns; he believed Thang Nhan would take over well! Of course I too urged Thang Nhan to obey his father. Dwelling in their house, I have always hoped the family might live together in harmony and peace.}
{align:justify|Speaking of the children, her cheeks suddenly flushed. (Naturally — reckoning by her present age, she is still so young, yet suddenly an older youth calls her mother; she must feel most unaccustomed!)}
{align:justify|Taking a keepsake photograph}
{align:justify|Before we knew it we had talked several hours; time to say farewell. I rose, took her hand, and comforted her:}
{align:justify|"All have treated you well, so you too should release and forget. Buddhism says all comes from gathered causes and conditions. Perhaps you and the Ngo family share affinity, that from distant Kinmen you drifted here to live among them."}
{align:justify|She nodded. I went on:}
{align:justify|"In any case, every day you sincerely recite the Buddha's name — wherever you live, practice just so. No need to reach a Recitation Hall. Buddhas and Bodhisattvas ever bless and shelter you! Moreover in the Buddhist spirit, benefit others first, self after. You have helped the whole household and made them joyful — this too is great merit!"}
{align:justify|She stayed silent, and I added:}
{align:justify|"If you wish to visit a Buddhist hall, when I am free I shall take you to Ho Vi for a visit. I hope from now you will be at peace and no longer often feel unbearable sorrow!"}
{align:justify|She grasped my hand, moved, thanking me again and again.}
{align:justify|Before leaving, I invited her to photograph with me as a keepsake. She seemed reluctant, till Thang Nhan coaxed and urged her long, and she nodded assent.}
{align:justify|As we left, Thang Nhan saw us off. On the road I asked the boy about his mother. He said:}
{align:justify|"My mother grew up in Mai Lieu from childhood and never went to Dai Tay or Kinmen. After she recovered she utterly changed into another person. I truly cannot believe such things. But the body is still my mother's — yet Mother steadfastly refuses to admit she is A Cuong. Kinfolk visiting — even Grandmother and the aunts — Mother recognizes none."}
{align:justify|The whole affair amazes the family. In my heart I feel somewhat wronged. I truly do not know what to call her.}
{align:justify|At this the boy paused. I asked:}
{align:justify|"And... now?"}
{align:justify|The boy smiled sadly:}
{align:justify|"Of course I still call her Mother — for it is my mother's body!"}
{align:justify|"Do you believe the 'borrowing a body to return in spirit'?"}
{align:justify|"Once I utterly disbelieved. My mother never went to Hai Phong islet, yet my present mother often describes Hai Phong islet. Moreover in the 48th year of the Republic I joined a camp in the Philippines with a friend from Kinmen, whose accent was most distinct."}
{align:justify|When I came home Mother was ill; once mended, her speech utterly changed — her accent exactly like that Kinmen friend's. Moreover she can tell a great many Kinmen tales. So I believe my later mother truly is a Kinmen person.}
{align:justify|Journalist Ly Ngoc's conclusion:}
{align:justify|I tell you readers this tale with no wish to stir idle curiosity to seek out Chu Tu Hoa. I only use this story to prove that Buddhism's "rebirth through the six realms, karmic retribution" is exact, and this principle ever holds.}
{align:justify|Lastly, here, I hope we shall all bless Chu Tu Hoa.}
{align:justify|Commentary:}
{align:justify|Every day we study the Buddha's teaching, ever reminded by Him: "This body is unreal, fragile, able to fall apart at any moment"... Yet we can hardly penetrate it, for from the moment we open our eyes at birth we already wear this body. But Chu Tu Hoa, in the very instant of "borrowing a body to return to life," returning to live, sees most easily that the body she wears is NOT hers.}
{align:justify|On the refugee road, being robbed and cast into the sea might be seen as old karmic debt, or a new cause the robber began to sow. But Chu Tu Hoa confided: "She understands the Buddha's teaching, believes in cause and effect, and bears no hatred toward her murderers — and when the robber's whole family died out, it was the reckoning of the indignant dead who voyaged with her."}
{align:justify|Chu Tu Hoa is a forgiving girl of high responsibility. Only an eighteen-year-old spirit, borrowing the body of a woman far older, with a son her own age — upon reviving, she must shoulder so much work: out to the fields at dawn, watching the paddies' water by night. Her husband's family runs construction and sells building materials besides. Where the text says "heavy hauling work, demanding strength, she does it all, shouldering everything" — suddenly I ache and pity her.}
{align:justify|Before, the former wife did but one thing: cook rice. But she, revived, embraces too much, shoulders too much, though none compels her. This is called high responsibility: living considerately, repaying kindness with kindness — for borrowing another's body, she must work through that body to repay the debt.}
{align:justify|Chu Tu Hoa's awakening is surely stronger than ours, for she has lived through life and death, through returning in spirit, and through living in a "borrowed" body in the truest sense.}
{align:justify|I have checked: she fled in the 43rd year of the Republic, that is 1954, and died that year. But only in the 48th year of the Republic, 1959, did she "borrow a body to return to life." So she spent five years dwelling in the yin realm. She says in those five years she stayed at Dao Hai Phong, its scenery lovely, its trees fair, and she liked it there very much. I believe she did not fall among ghosts, for her heart harbored no anger or hatred — her heart was most kind, and realms ever match the heart. So though in the yin realm, she describes it leisurely, as one content.}
{align:justify|Until the spirits told her that Mrs. A Duong was ill, her lifespan nearly spent, and she might "borrow the body to return to life" — only then did she seek out Mr. Ngo, "ride" along, and hover round him, waiting for him to go home so she might follow and enter the body.}
{align:justify|Chu Tu Hoa had to enter a body twice, half again her own age — unwed, yet made wife and mother. Revived, never again to meet the father and mother her heart remembered (parting in life hurts worse than parting in death), she must shoulder a new family in poorer estate. Yet she still fulfills every duty and obligation.}
{align:justify|After returning in spirit, Chu Tu Hoa once had the family light incense and set chairs to talk with her friends. These friends may be spirits of the yin realm, fellow dead who perished with her at sea — and guardian deities too... But fine it is that they came to comfort her only once after her borrowing, then never again. Meaning the two realms are sharply divided; let each live in peace in their own realm. This too is the finest mutual shelter.}
{align:justify|Eighteen years old, yet Chu Tu Hoa's experience exceeds ours by far — for she has been human and ghost, lived through theyin realm, through living in a "borrowed" body in the truest sense.}
{align:justify|All this has kindled the lamp of awakening high in her, so revived, she hastens to invite Buddha and Bodhisattva statues for worship, morning and evening diligent in obeisance without cease, steadfastly vegetarian, longing to ascend a temple Recitation Hall. All this I understand. And surely readers too share fellow feeling with her, each in their own way.}
{align:justify|I translate this piece not to amuse readers, but that we may share more fine things of transmigration's teaching, of the realms... and understand the Buddha's words never deceive.}
{align:justify|Journalist Ly Ngoc once hoped we would bless Chu Tu Hoa after reading this tale. I guess Chu Tu Hoa must be old now, of the "seventy years rare from olden times" age (?) — and none knows whether she lives or has passed?}
{align:justify|For myself, before closing this book, I would pray blessings... not for Chu Tu Hoa alone, but for all readers — wishing you all everlasting peace, ever knowing how to live so that, in yin realm or bright, you are happy forever.}
{align:justify|Translated 29/3/2013}
{align:justify|Hanh Doan}
{align:justify|NOTES}
{align:justify|Correctly translated means "bestowed."}
{align:justify|Real names have been changed.}
{align:justify|Married women in China are named in the style with the husband's surname first — Mr. Ngo's wife was born Lam.}
